CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to contribute to enhancing the cultural life of the people and promoting the national culture by upgrading the motion pictures and video products qualitatively and promoting the development of the film and video culture and its industry. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2016>

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Jun. 5, 2008; May 8, 2009; Feb. 17, 2012; May 18, 2015; Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023; Oct. 31, 2023>
  1. "Motion picture" means any work, the successive pictures of which are contained in any digital medium, such as films, disks, etc., which is produced for the purpose of public viewing at any place or establishment, such as movie theaters;
  2. "Film industry" means an industry engaged in the production, utilization, distribution, dissemination, exportation, importation, etc. of motion pictures;
  3. "Korean motion picture" means a motion picture produced by persons (including corporations) headquartered in Korea, and other motion pictures deemed Korean motion pictures pursuant to Article 27;
  4. "Jointly produced motion picture" means a motion picture produced jointly by Korean and foreign motion picture makers so as to cover the production costs through their joint financing, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism;
  5. "Animation film" means a motion picture that fictionalizes a real world or an imaginary world to allow viewers to feel the dynamic sense similar to the reality through the utilization of human resources or technology;
  6. "Small film" means a motion picture produced by using film not exceeding 16 millimeters or any digital medium, which is prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism;
  7. "Short film" means a motion picture with a running time not exceeding 40 minutes;
  8. "Screening" means presenting motion pictures for public viewing on screen;
  9. "Motion picture enterpriser" means any of the following persons having a profit-making purpose:
  a. Motion picture makers: Persons who operate business making motion pictures;
  b. Motion picture importers: Persons who operate business importing motion pictures;
  c. Motion picture distributors: Persons who operate business distributing motion pictures;
  d. Motion picture screeners: Persons who operate business screening motion pictures;
  10. "Movie theater" means any place or establishment where motion pictures are screened for profit; provided, such place and establishment (hereinafter referred to as "non-permanent movie theater") shall be excluded, where motion pictures are screened for a period not exceeding the number of days per year prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  11. "Restricted-screening theater" means a movie theater where restricted motion pictures provided for in Article 29(2)5 are screened;
  12. "Video product" means any work, the successive pictures of which are contained in any digital medium or device, such as tapes or disks, which is produced to be viewed or to be viewed and heard by being played through machines or electrical, electronic, or communication apparatus; provided, any of the following products shall be excluded:
  a. Game software products provided in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Game Industry Promotion Act;
  b. Video products operated on computer programs (limited to those without any recorded motion picture);
  12-2. "Online video product" means video products produced to be provided for viewing via an information and communications network defined in Article 2(1)1 of the Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Network Utilization and Information Protection (hereinafter referred to as "information and communications network");
  13. "Video industry" means an industry engaged in the production, utilization, distribution, supply, export, import, etc. of video products;
  14. "Video product manufacturing business" means any business manufacturing or reproducing video products;
  15. "Video product distribution business" means any business importing video products (including the originals of the video products) or reserving or managing copyrights therein to supply retailers or lenders with video products;
  16. "Video service providing business" means any of the following businesses:
  a. The business of running a video-viewing establishment: Business offering video products to the public for the purpose of their viewing with multiple partitioned video-viewing compartments and video-viewing apparatuses installed therein (including where customers operate the viewing machine by themselves);
  b. The business of running a video-viewing mini-theater: Business specializing in video products offered to the public for the purpose of their viewing, with screens, a number of viewers’ seats, and video-viewing apparatuses installed therein;
  c. The business of running a video-viewing mini-theater for video products with a restricted rating: Business exclusively offering restricted-rated video products only to the public, for viewing on screens, a number of spectator's seats, and video-viewing apparatuses installed therein;
  d. The business of providing multiple types of visual materials: Business offering incidental facilities for playing with game software or singing to the public, running a video-viewing establishment;
  e. Other business of rendering video service: Business offering video products to the public for viewing at places or establishments used for public accommodation, rest, etc. on video-viewing apparatuses installed therein;
  17. "Video product business operator" means a person who runs any business referred to in subparagraphs 14 through 16;
  18. "Youth" means a youth defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Youth Protection Act;
  19. "Digital cinema" means showing, to the public, a film work processed in digital file format using a digital projector or telecommunications apparatus via digital media, such as disks, or information and communications network, or providing a user with such film work;
  20. "Content information" means the extent of the theme, obscenity, violence, language, fear factor, drug use, risk of imitation, etc., concerning the contents of a film or video product, and other information related thereto;
  21. "Motion picture worker" means a person prescribed by Presidential Decree, engaged in the film industry;
  22. "Motion picture enterprisers' organization" means an organization of motion picture enterprisers which has the authority to control or regulate the motion picture enterprisers who are the members thereof;
  23. "Motion picture workers union" means an organization formed by motion picture workers as its principal agents for the purpose of maintaining or improving working conditions and promoting the improvement of other economic and social positions of motion picture workers by uniting independently, or a federation thereof.

CHAPTER II MOTION PICTURES

SECTION 1 Formulation and Implementation of Master Plans for Promoting Motion Pictures and Creation of Fair Environments

Article 3 (Master plans for promoting motion pictures and plans for implementation thereof) (1) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism shall formulate and implement a master plan for promoting motion pictures and an implementation plan thereof to enhance the film and video culture and to develop the film and video industry, upon recommendation by the Korean Film Council established pursuant to Article 4 (hereinafter referred to as the "Korean Film Council") and the Korean Film Archive established pursuant to Article 34 (hereinafter referred to as the "Korean Film Archive"). <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) The master plan for promoting motion pictures under paragraph (1) shall contain the following matters: <Amended on Jun. 5, 2008; Feb. 17, 2012; May 18, 2015; Feb. 3, 2016; Mar. 13, 2018; May 18, 2021; Dec. 28, 2021; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Basic direction-setting for promoting Korean motion pictures;
  2. Surveys and research for promoting the production of motion pictures, establishment of the motion picture-making infrastructure, and development of related technologies;
  3. Surveys, research, and development for promoting the distribution of motion pictures and developing exhibition systems;
  4. Exports and international marketing of Korean motion pictures;
  5. Collecting and preserving film materials;
  6. Developing human resources in the film industry and improving motion picture workers' working environments;
  6-2. Raising awareness of respect for human rights and gender equality in the film industry;
  6-3. Cultivating and supporting art films and independent films;
  7. Securing financial resources to expand the financial basis necessary for the promotion of motion pictures and devising ways to efficiently manage such resources;
  8. International exchange and cooperation in the field of motion pictures;
  9. Basic direction-setting for promoting digital cinemas, creation of infrastructure for the digital cinema industry, and measures for financing and efficient operation thereof;
  10. Developing and standardizing film technology, quality certification of digital cinemas, and facility standards for, etc.;
  11. Facilitating the diversification and publicness of the film and video culture;
  11-2. Promoting local film and video culture;
  12. Matters concerning the safety, sanitation, and disease control with respect to infectious diseases, etc. in movie theaters;
  13. Other necessary matters to promote movie arts.
  (3) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may prescribe matters concerning technical standards, quality certification, facility standards, etc. related to paragraph (2) 10 for the development of the digital cinema, and recommend them to relevant enterprises, such as motion picture enterprises; provided, where the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism intends to prescribe the technical standards, the Minister shall consult with the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources. <Added on Jun. 5, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 3-2 (Labor-managerial-governmental council for motion pictures) In order to promote the film industry and to improve working environments of motion picture workers, the representatives of motion picture workers unions and motion picture enterprisers or motion picture enterprisers' organization and the Government may organize a labor-managerial-governmental council for motion pictures.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

Article 3-3 (Guidelines on standard wages) (1) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism shall prepare guidelines on standard wages of motion picture workers ("wages" means all compensation received by motion picture workers in return for their services; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) in consultation with the labor-managerial-governmental council prescribed in Article 3-2, disseminate them, and recommend to implement them.
  (2) Motion picture enterprisers and motion picture workers shall comply with the guidelines on standard wages.
  (3) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may cause the Korean Film Council to conduct a fact-finding survey or research on guidelines on standard wages. In such cases, a person in receipt of a request to submit data for the fact-finding survey shall comply with such request, except in extenuating circumstances.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

Article 3-4 (Specification of working conditions) When a motion picture enterprise concludes a contract with a motion picture worker, the motion picture enterpriser shall specify the wage, working hours, and other working conditions of the motion picture worker in detail.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

Article 3-5 (Use and dissemination of standard-form contract) (1) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may recommend motion picture enterprisers or motion picture enterprisers' organizations to prepare and use a standard-form contract.
  (2) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism and the Korean Film Council may give preference to motion picture enterprisers or motion picture enterprisers' organizations using a standard-form contract referred to in paragraph (1), in the provision of financial support for film and video product industries (including investment in cultural industries made by investment associations funded under the Framework Act on the Promotion of Cultural Industries in accordance with Articles 8 and 9 of the same Act), such as support for the Motion Picture Development Fund under Article 23 of this Act. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

Article 3-6 (Protection from accidents) (1) Motion picture enterprisers shall take appropriate measures to protect motion picture workers from accidents occurring in the course of filming motion pictures.
  (2) The State may provide support for the measures referred to in paragraph (1), within budgetary limits.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

Article 3-7 (Preventive measures such as sexual violence prevention education) (1) Motion picture makers shall take measures necessary to prevent sexual violence, such as requiring those participating in filming to receive sexual violence prevention education before filming motion pictures.
  (2) The Korean Film Council may provide necessary support, such as developing programs for sexual violence prevention education and training instructors, for taking the measures under paragraph (1).
  (3) The State may subsidize expenses incurred in taking measures under paragraph (1) and providing support under paragraph (2), within budgetary limits.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2021]
  [Previous Article 3-7 moved to Article 3-8 <May 18, 2021>]

Article 3-8 (Execution of vocational training) (1) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may execute vocational training for motion picture workers who wish to obtain vocational training.
  (2) In order to improve the efficiency of vocational training prescribed in paragraph (1), the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may outsource the execution of vocational training to a vocational education and training institution or a person capable of executing vocational education and training. In such cases, the Vocational Education and Training Promotion Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the matters necessary for outsourcing, etc. vocational training.
  (3) The State may provide financial support necessary for the execution of vocational training under paragraph (1) and pay a vocational training allowance to motion picture workers who participate in vocational training, within budgetary limits.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]
  [Moved from Article 3-7; previous Article 3-8 moved to Article 3-9 <May 18, 2021>]

Article 3-9 (Sanctions against delayed payment of wages) If any motion picture enterpriser delays payment of wages of motion picture workers during the production period of a motion picture, violates Article 3-4, or fails to use a standard-form contract referred to in Article 3-5(1), the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism and the Korean Film Council may exclude such motion picture enterprise from projects executed with financial support for film and video product industries (including investment in cultural industries made by investment associations funded under the Framework Act on the Promotion of Cultural Industries in accordance with Articles 8 and 9 of the same Act), such as the support of the Motion Picture Development Fund under Article 23 of this Act.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]
  [Moved from Article 3-8 <May 18, 2021>]

Article 3-10 (Fact-finding surveys) (1) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may conduct a fact-finding survey to promote the film industry or to improve the working environment of motion picture workers.
  (2) Where necessary to conduct a fact-finding survey under paragraph (1), the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may require the relevant public institution or a related corporation, organization, or individual to submit data. In such cases, a person in receipt of a request to submit data shall comply with such request, except in extenuating circumstances.
  (3) Matters necessary for the details of, scope of, and procedures, etc. for, fact-finding surveys under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2021]

SECTION 2 Korean Film Council

Article 4 (Establishment) The Korean Film Council shall be established under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism in order to devise a qualitative enhancement of motion pictures and to promote Korean motion pictures and the film industry. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>

Article 5 (Corporate personality) The Korean Film Council shall be a juristic person.

Article 6 (Articles of association) (1) The articles of association of the Korean Film Council shall contain the following: <Amended on Feb. 17, 2012>
  1. Objectives;
  2. Name;
  3. Matters related to the principal office;
  4. Matters related to executive officers (referring to the members, including the chairperson and vice-chairperson of the Korean Film Council, and auditors thereof; hereafter the same shall apply in this paragraph) and employees;
  5. Matters related to standards for remuneration for executive officers;
  6. Scope and details of business and matters related to the execution thereof;
  7. Matters related to the property and accounting;
  8. Matters related to organization and structure;
  9. Matters related to the alteration in the articles of association;
  10. Matters related to dissolution;
  11. Other matters related to the operation of the Council.
  (2) When preparing or altering the articles of association, the Council shall obtain authorization from the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Added on Feb. 17, 2012>

Article 7 (Registration) (1) The Korean Film Council shall be deemed to be established by registering its establishment at the location of its principal office.
  (2) Necessary matters concerning the establishment registration of the Korean Film Council shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 8 (Composition of Korean Film Council) (1) The Korean Film Council shall consist of nine members including one chairperson and one vice chairperson.
  (2) The members of the Korean Film Council shall be appointed by the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism, from among persons with expertise and abundant experience in the movie arts, the film industry, etc., taking into account the balance of their gender, age, expertise, etc.; provided, three or more motion picture enterprisers shall not be included as its members. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Feb. 17, 2012; Jul. 16, 2013; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (3) The chairperson and the vice-chairperson of the Korean Film Council shall be elected from among its members. <Amended on Feb. 17, 2012; Jun. 9, 2020>
  (4) Necessary matters concerning the composition and operation of the Korean Film Council including the standards, etc. for nomination of its members shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 9 (Chairperson' duties) (1) The chairperson of the Korean Film Council shall represent the Korean Film Council, and exercise overall control over its affairs.
  (2) If the chairperson of the Korean Film Council is unable to perform his or her duties due to unavoidable reasons, the vice chairperson shall act on behalf of the chairperson, and if both the chairperson and the vice chairperson are unable to perform their duties, a member named in order of seniority shall act on behalf of the chairperson.
  (3) Members other than the chairperson of the Korean Film Council shall be non-permanent.

Article 10 (Term of office of Committee members) (1) The members of the Korean Film Council including the chairperson shall hold office for a term of three years. <Amended on Feb. 17, 2012; Jun. 9, 2020>
  (2) The chairperson and members of the Korean Film Council may be consecutively appointed only once. <Added on Feb. 17, 2012; Jun. 9, 2020>
  (3) Where the office of any member of the Korean Film Council is vacant, the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism shall appoint a supplementary member. In such cases, the term of office of the supplementary member shall be the remaining period of the term of office of his or her predecessor. <Amended on Feb. 17, 2012; Jun. 9, 2020>
  (4) The members of the Korean Film Council, including its chairperson and vice chairperson, whose term of office has expired pursuant to paragraph (1) and their successors are not yet appointed, shall continue to perform their duties until their successors are to be appointed. <Amended on Feb. 17, 2012; Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 11 Deleted. <Feb. 17, 2012>

Article 12 (Disqualifications for commissioners) Any of the following persons shall be disqualified for being a member of the Korean Film Council: <Amended on Feb. 17, 2012; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. A public official (excluding a public educational official under the Public Educational Officials Act and a judicial officer);
  2. A member of a political party under the Political Parties Act;
  3. Any person falling under any subparagraph of Article 33 of the State Public Officials Act;
  4. Deleted; <Jul. 16, 2013>
  5. A video product business operator who has filed a report or registration pursuant to Article 57(1) or 58(1);
  6. A person under Article 34(1)2 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions.

Article 12-2 (Prohibition on participation) No member of the Korean Film Council shall participate in deliberations and resolutions on matters related to the interests of the person in question or any person with whom he or she has kinship under Article 777 of the Civil Act.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 17, 2012]

Article 13 (Independence of members in performing their duties, and guarantee of their status) (1) The members of the Korean Film Council shall be free from any instructions or interventions while performing their duties during their terms of office.
  (2) The members of the Korean Film Council shall not be dismissed against their will, except for the cases falling under each of the following subparagraphs:
  1. Where he or she falls under any disqualification referred to in any subparagraph of Article 12;
  2. Where he or she becomes unable to perform the duties for a long time due to a physical or mental impairment.

Article 14 (Korean Film Council's functions) (1) The Korean Film Council shall deliberate on, and resolve, the following matters: <Amended on Jan. 26, 2007; Jun. 5, 2008; Feb. 3, 2016; Mar. 13, 2018; May 18, 2021; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Presentation of views on the formulation and alteration of master plans, etc. for the promotion of motion pictures;
  2. Formulation and implementation of operational plans for the Korean Film Council;
  3. Enactment, amendment and repeal of the Korean Film Council’s articles of association and rules;
  4. Management and operation of facilities related to the production of films;
  5. Management and operation of the Motion Picture Development Fund under Article 23;
  6. Survey, research, education and training for promoting Korean motion pictures and fostering the film industry;
  7. Support for the distribution of motion pictures;
  7-2. Matters concerning the development of film technology related to digital cinema, establishment and dissemination of standards therefor, quality certification, facility standards, etc. of movie theaters, etc.;
  8. International marketing and exchanges of Korean motion pictures;
  9. Development of art films, independent films, animation films, small films and short films;
  2. Animation films, small films and short films, or art films or independent films recognized by the Korean Film Council;
  11. Raising of awareness of respect for human rights and gender equality in the film industry;
  12. Recognition of co-produced motion pictures as Korean motion pictures under Article 27;
  13. Operation of the integrated computer network for movie theater admission tickets under Article 39;
  14. Operation and improvement of the screen quota system for Korean motion pictures under Article 40;
  14-2. Promotion of local film and video culture;
  15. Implementation of policies for promoting the video industry;
  16. Encouragement of wider use of a standard form contract and improvement of working environment in the film industry;
  17. Other matters deemed necessary by the Korean Film Council.
  (2) The Korean Film Council shall be deemed as the deliberative committee on fund management under Article 74(1) of the National Finance Act, when it deliberates on an important matter related to management and operation of the Motion Picture Development Fund pursuant to paragraph (1)5. <Added on Jan. 26, 2007>

Article 15 (Quorum for resolution) The Korean Film Council shall adopt resolutions with the affirmative vote of a majority of the incumbent members. <Amended on Jan. 26, 2007; Feb. 17, 2012>

Article 16 (Meetings open to public) (1) Meetings of the Korean Film Council shall be made open to the public as prescribed by the Regulations of the Korean Film Council; provided, the same shall not apply to the case where the Korean Film Council has resolved otherwise because it is deemed especially necessary to conduct its affairs fairly. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) The Korean Film Council shall prepare minutes of its meetings, as prescribed by the Regulations of the Korean Film Council. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 17 (Sectional committees) (1) The Korean Film Council may compose and operate sectional committees to perform its duties under Article 14, and matters necessary to compose and operate them shall be prescribed by the Korean Film Council's rules. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) Any member who is a motion picture enterpriser shall not be a member of a sectional committee directly related to the field in which he or she runs business by himself or herself among the fields classified in items of subparagraph 9 of Article 2. <Added on Jul. 16, 2013>
  (3) The Korean Film Council may establish and operate a research institution to perform the duties under Article 14(1)6, and matters necessary for the establishment and operation thereof shall be prescribed by the Korean Film Council's rules. <Amended on Jan. 26, 2007; Jul. 16, 2013>

Article 18 (Compilation of budget) (1) The Korean Film Council shall obtain approval of the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism for the basic direction and scale for the compilation of annual budget, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may request the Korean Film Council to furnish the data necessary for the business plans, budget and the settlement of accounts of the Korean Film Council. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>

Article 19 (Auditor) (1) The Korean Film Council shall have one auditor in order to audit matters related to its business and accounting.
  (2) The auditor shall be appointed by the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism and shall be a non-standing auditor. <Amended on Feb. 17, 2012; Jun. 9, 2020>
  (3) The auditor shall hold office for a term of three years and may be consecutively appointed only once. <Amended on Feb. 17, 2012; Jun. 9, 2020>

Article 20 (Secretariat) (1) The Korean Film Council shall have a secretariat in order to assist in the handling of the Korean Film Council's affairs.
  (2) The secretariat shall have one secretary-general, and the chairperson of the Korean Film Council shall appoint the secretary-general with the consent of the Korean Film Council.

Article 21 (Enactment and amendment of Korean Film Council's Rules) The Korean Film Council shall, when it intends to enact, amend or repeal its rules, make a public notification thereof on the Internet, etc. not less than 20 days in advance, and when it has enacted, amended or repealed its rules, the Korean Film Council shall without delay publish it on the Internet, etc. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 22 (Assistance by National Treasury) Expenses necessary to operate the Korean Film Council may be assisted by the National Treasury.

SECTION 3 Motion Picture Development Fund

Article 23 (Establishment of Fund) (1) The Motion Picture Development Fund (hereinafter referred to as the "Fund") shall be established in order to improve motion pictures qualitatively and to promote and develop Korean motion pictures and the film and video product industries.
  (2) The Fund shall be managed and operated by the Korean Film Council under Article 4; and shall have a separate account from the Korean Film Council’s account. <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018>
  (3) Matters necessary for the management and operation of the Fund shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jan. 26, 2007]

Article 24 (Raising of Fund) The Fund shall be raised from financial resources falling under each of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Apr. 11, 2007; Aug. 8, 2023; Dec. 31, 2024; Mar. 25, 2025>
  1. Contributions from the Government;
  2. Contributions from private individuals and legal entities;
  3. Charges on movie tickets under Article 25-2;
  4. Earnings from the management of the Fund;
  5. Other earnings prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jan. 26, 2007]

Article 25 (Uses of Funds) (1) Funds from the Fund shall be used for any of the following projects: <Amended on Jan. 26, 2007; Jun. 5, 2008; Feb. 17, 2012; Feb. 3, 2016; Mar. 13, 2018; May 18, 2021; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Support for the promotion of creation and production of Korean motion pictures;
  2. Investment in investment associations specializing in motion pictures;
  3. Support for the exports and international exchanges of Korean motion pictures;
  4. Support for the production of small or short films;
  4-2. Support for exclusive movie theaters under Article 38(1);
  5. Support for the repair, maintenance and renovation of the facilities of movie theaters;
  5-2. Support for projects to improve the welfare of workers in the film industry;
  5-3. Support for projects for raising awareness of human rights and gender equality in the film industry;
  6. Support for film-related projects of film-related organizations and civic groups recognized by the Korean Film Council;
  6-2. Support for education, training, etc. related to motion pictures;
  7. Support for the development of art films and independent films;
  8. Support for the promotion of diversification and publicness of the film and video culture;
  8-2. Support for the development of film technology;
  8-3. Support for the promotion of local film and video culture;
  9. Support for the promotion of the video industry;
  10. Support for the promotion of the rights of persons with disabilities and other cultural minority groups to enjoy movies;
  10-2. Support for the encouragement of the inter-Korean exchanges in motion pictures;
  11. Expenses for raising, operating, and managing the Fund;
  12. Support for a project that the Korean Film Council determines as necessary to promote the film and video industry.
  (2) The amount of funds for the project under paragraph (1)12 shall not exceed 15/100 of the annual expenditure of the Fund. <Amended on Jan. 26, 2007>

Article 25-2 (Collection of charges) (1) The Korean Film Council shall collect an amount equivalent to 3/100 of the admission ticket value from audiences entering a movie theater (including non-permanent movie theaters; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) as a charge for the development of Korean films and the promotion of the movie and video product industries; provided, this shall not apply to audiences entering any of the following movie theaters:
  1. A movie theater that has screened any motion picture falling under Article 38(1)2, for a period of at least 60/100 of the annual running days in the immediately preceding year;
  2. A movie theater, the movie ticket sales of which (where at least 2 movie theaters are operated, referring to the aggregate movie tickets sales of each movie theater; and where the period of business in the immediately preceding year is shorter than 1 year, referring to an amount calculated by converting the sales of movie tickets in the relevant period to annual sales) in the immediately preceding year is less than the amount prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  3. Movie theaters whose monthly sales of tickets decreased due to the outbreak of infectious disease under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Infectious Disease Control and Prevention Act by more than a percentage prescribed by Presidential Decree (where two or more movie theaters are operated, it shall be based on the total amount of ticket sales for each movie theater) compared to the average sales of tickets in the same month for the three years (if the business period is less than three years, referring to the business period) immediately before the outbreak of infectious diseases; provided, if the business days of the same month for the three years immediately preceding the year of the outbreak of the infectious disease are less than one month, the amount of ticket sales for the month shall be based on the amount converted into monthly sales; if there are no business days of the same month, the average monthly ticket sales during the business period up to the year immediately before the outbreak of the infectious disease shall be based on the average monthly ticket sales.
  (2) Every movie theater operator shall collect the charges under paragraph (1) from the audiences and pay the relevant charges to the Korean Film Council by payment deadline prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) Whenever paying charges collected under paragraph (2), every movie theater operator shall submit the materials related to the receipt of such charges, including a copy of the charge receipt ledger, to the Korean Film Council; provided, if the Korean Film Council is able to verify the charges that should be collected by every movie theater operator through the integrated computer network for processing movie theater admission tickets referred to in Article 39(1), no materials related to the receipt of such charges need be submitted.
  (4) Where a movie theater operator fails to pay the charges received from audiences under paragraph (2) by the deadline for payment, the Korean Film Council may impose an amount equivalent to 3/100 of the amount in arrears as additional dues.
  (5) Where a movie theater operator fails to pay the charges under paragraph (2) and the additional dues under paragraph (4) by the payment deadline, the Korean Film Council may collect them in the same manner as national taxes are compulsorily collected.
  (6) Matters necessary for the method of collecting charges and additional dues and the payment deadline thereof, submission of materials related to the receipt of charges, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (7) The Korean Film Council may pay commission fees for the receipt of charges by the operators of movie theaters under paragraph (2) with approval from the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism. In such cases, the fees shall not exceed 3 percent of the amount of charges collected under paragraph (1).
  [This Article Added on Mar. 25, 2025]

Article 25-3 (Evaluation of performance) (1) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism shall measure and evaluate the performance of spending the Fund for the projects under subparagraphs of Article 25(1), and shall notify the Korean Film Council of the results thereof no later than March 31 next year. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism shall establish the target for performance and the guidelines for evaluation subject to the consultation with the Korean Film Council for measuring and evaluating the performance under paragraph (1). <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
  (3) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may, if it is deemed as a result of the evaluation of performance under paragraph (1) that there is a matter to be corrected, demand the Korean Film Council to correct such a matter. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (4) Matters necessary for the method, procedure, etc. for the evaluation of performance shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 26, 2007]

SECTION 4 Report of Motion Picture Enterprisers

Article 26 (Report of motion picture enterprisers) (1) A person who intends to become a motion picture enterpriser shall report his or her business to the Mayor of a Special Self-Governing City, the Governor of a Special Self-Governing Province or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu (the head of a Gu refers to the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter referred to as "head of a Si/Gun/Gu"). The same shall also apply to where he or she alters the reported matters. <Amended on May 8, 2009; May 18, 2015>
  (2) Deleted. <Mar. 25, 2025>
  (3) Matters necessary concerning procedures for the report and altered report under paragraph (1), the issuance and re-issuance of report certificates, etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 24, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 27 (Recognition of co-produced motion pictures as Korean motion pictures) (1) A co-produced motion picture may be recognized as a Korean motion picture if human or material factors involved in the production of the relevant motion picture or the artistic or technical features of the relevant motion picture meet the standards for recognition as Korean motion picture. <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018>
  (2) A person who intends to have a co-produced motion picture recognized as a Korean motion picture under paragraph (1) shall file an application therefor with the Korean Film Council.
  (3) Matters necessary for the procedures and methods of recognition, the standards for recognition as Korean motion pictures, etc. under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (4) Where a co-produced motion picture recognized as a Korean motion picture ceases to meet the standards for recognition as Korean motion pictures after completion of the co-production, the Korean Film Council may cancel the recognition of the co-produced motion picture as a Korean motion picture. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 28 (Supply and distribution of motion pictures) (1) When a motion picture enterpriser supplies motion pictures to any other motion picture enterpriser, the former shall supply the latter with such motion pictures at fair and rational market prices without discrimination.
  (2) Were the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism makes a judgment that the distribution order of motion pictures is in violation of Article 5(1), 40(1), 45(1), 46 or 51(1) of the Monopoly Regulation and Fair Trade Act, he or she shall notify the Fair Trade Commission of the fact. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 29, 2020>
  (3) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism shall endeavor to prevent the author's property rights of a motion picture enterpriser and the rights protected under the Copyright Act from being infringed on in the course of manufacturing, screening and distributing the motion picture. <Added on Mar. 17, 2010>

Article 28-2 (Reporting on business closure and ex officio cancellation) (1) Where any person who has reported under Article 26 closes his or her business, he or she shall report on business closure with the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu within 30 days from the date of business closure, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Dec. 28, 2021>
  (2) Where any person subject to reporting on business closure under paragraph (1) fails to report thereon, the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu may ex officio cancel the reported matters after verifying the business closure, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

Article 28-3 (Assistance in shooting of visual materials) (1) Where necessary for the promotion of the film and video industries, the State and local governments may provide assistance in filming visual materials, including provision of local sets for filming in Korea.
  (2) The head of a local government may request cooperation to the heads of relevant administrative agencies, to assist in filming visual materials under paragraph (1). In such cases, the heads of the relevant administrative agencies in receipt of such request shall provide cooperation, except in extenuating circumstances.
  (3) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may determine and publicly announce standards for cooperation in shooting of visual materials, following consultation with the heads of relevant administrative agencies.
  (4) Matters necessary for providing assistance under paragraph (1) and requesting cooperation under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by municipal ordinance of the relevant local government in accordance with the standards prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

Article 28-4 (Film commission) (1) In order to perform the following affairs, the Special Metropolitan City, a Metropolitan city, a Special Self-Governing City, a Do and a Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as "City/Do") may establish a film commission:
  1. Invitation of and support in film and video production and shooting;
  2. Operation of facilities related to film and video production;
  3. Provision of sets for filming films and videos and information related thereto;
  4. Promotion of regional film and video culture;
  5. Utilization of facilities related to films and videos and sets for filming as tourist attractions;
  6. Other matters prescribed by municipal ordinance of the relevant City/Do.
  (2) A local government may subsidize expenses incurred in the operation of the film commission, within budgetary limits.
  (3) Matters necessary for the organization and operation of the film commission shall be prescribed by municipal ordinance of the relevant City/Do, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

Article 28-5 (International motion picture festivals) (1) For the development of video culture, industry and regional economy, a local government may give necessary support to a corporation or organization holding an international motion picture festival or hosting and operating an international motion picture conference, for the following projects:
  1. Invitation and screening of domestic and foreign motion pictures at international motion picture festivals;
  2. Producing domestic and foreign motion pictures;
  3. Awarding domestic and international motion pictures;
  4. Utilization of local video-related facilities and filming sites;
  5. Other matters prescribed by Ordinance of the relevant local government.
  (2) The State and local governments may subsidize expenses incurred in hosting and operating international motion picture festivals within budgetary limits.
  (3) Matters necessary for the holding, operation, and support of international motion picture festivals shall be prescribed by ordinance of the relevant local government, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism.
  [This Article Added on, Dec. 28, 2021]

SECTION 5 Film Rating and Restriction on Advertisement and Publicity

Article 29 (Film rating) (1) A motion picture enterpriser who has produced or imported a motion picture (including the trailers and advertising films), shall have the motion picture rated by the Korea Media Rating Board referred to in Article 71 (hereinafter referred to as the "Korea Media Rating Board") before the screening of such motion picture; provided, the same shall not apply to the following motion pictures: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 8, 2023; Mar. 25, 2025>
  1. Small films and short films to be screened free of charge at a specific place, exclusively for specific persons not including youth;
  2. Motion pictures to be screened in the film festivals recommended by the Korean Film Council;
  2-2. Motion pictures to be imported or re-screened with the same content of the motion pictures that have been rated;
  3. Any motion picture recognized by the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism that the rating is unnecessary, such as those for international cultural exchanges.
  (2) The rating for motion pictures under the main clause of paragraph (1) shall be classified as detailed in the following subparagraphs based on the contents of a motion picture and the degree of expression, etc.; provided, advertising films to be screened before or after the screening of motion pictures shall be rated only when they fall under subparagraph 1, and trailers shall be rated according to subparagraph 1 or 4, and trailers rated "adults only" shall be seen only before or after the screening of motion pictures rated "adults only": <Amended on May 8, 2009; Feb. 17, 2012; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. All ages admitted: Motion pictures that all ages are permitted to view;
  2. 12 years or older admitted: Motion pictures that persons aged 12 or older are permitted to view;
  3. 15 years or older admitted: Motion pictures that persons aged 15 or older are permitted to view;
  4. Adults only: Motion pictures that no one under 18 is permitted to view;
  5. Restricted: Motion pictures that need to be restricted to a certain extent in its screening, advertising and publicity, as they excessively express lewdness, violence, social acts, etc. and thus are likely to markedly hamper the universal human dignity, social values, good morals, or national sentiment.
  (3) No one shall screen motion pictures that have not been rated, in violation of paragraphs (1) and (2).
  (4) In cases of a motion pictures with any rating provided in paragraph (2)2 or 3, no one shall admit any person under the age permitted to view the relevant motion picture; provided, the same shall not apply where such person is accompanied by his or her parents or other guardians. <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (5) In cases of a motion picture with any rating provided in paragraph (2)4 or 5, no one shall admit any youth to view such motion picture. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (6) No one shall alter a rating under paragraph (1) or screen a motion picture by altering its contents differently from the rated version. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (7) The detailed criteria for the rating under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree based on the following matters: <Added on May 8, 2009>
  1. Matters concerning the maintenance of the democratic basic order and the respect for human rights pursuant to the Constitution of the Republic of Korea;
  2. Matters concerning the sound domesticity and the protection of children and youth;
  3. Matters concerning the respect for social ethics;
  4. Matters concerning the maintenance of the national identity and diplomatic relations;
  5. Matters concerning the violence, lewdness, anti-social acts, etc. of motion pictures in their themes and contents;
  6. Matters concerning the universal human dignity and social values, good morals, and national sentiment.
  (8) The Korea Media Rating Board shall deliberate on any matter concerning the provision of content information when rating a motion picture pursuant to paragraph (1). <Added on May 8, 2009>
  (9) The Korea Media Rating Board shall issue the following documents to the applicant when it has rated a motion picture: <Added on May 8, 2009; Oct. 16, 2018>
  1. A film rating certificate stating the rating and content information of the motion picture;
  2. Documents stating duties to be fulfilled according to the rating.
  (10) Procedures and methods for the rating and the provision of content information, procedures for issuing film rating certificates, and other necessary matters under paragraphs (1), (8), and (9) shall be determined by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Added on May 8, 2009; Oct. 16, 2018>
  [Paragraph (2)5 of this Article was amended by Act No. 9657 promulgated on May 8, 2009, pursuant to the decision of inconsistency with the Constitution by the Constitutional Court on July 31, 2008.]

Article 30 (Request for presentation of identification cards for confirmation of age of viewers) In order to comply with the main clause of Article 29(4) or paragraph (5) of the same Article, a person who intends to have an audience visitor enter the place may request the presentation of his or her resident registration certificate (including a mobile resident registration certificate) or other certificate verifying his or her age, if it is necessary to verify the age of the audience visitor, and may restrict the admission of a person who fails to present the certificate without justifiable grounds.
  [This Article Added on Oct. 22, 2024]

Article 31 (Re-rating of film rating) (1) A motion picture enterpriser who is dissatisfied with the rating of his or her motion picture granted under Article 29 may raise an objection to the Korea Media Rating Board to review the rating, specifying the reasons therefor, within 30 days from the day when it has been rated. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) The Korea Media Rating Board shall examine an objection raised under paragraph (1), and if the objection is well-grounded, shall notify the applicant or his or her agent of the rating reclassified within 15 days from the date of receipt of the objection; if the objection is not well-grounded, shall notify the applicant or his or her agent thereof. In such cases, the period for notification shall not be extended. <Amended on May. 16, 2023>
  (3) Matters necessary for the procedures and methods for filing an objection under paragraphs (1) and (2), the notification thereof, etc. shall be prescribed by the Regulations of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Amended on May. 16, 2023>

Article 32 (Restrictions on distribution and posting of advertising or publicity materials) (1) A person who intends to distribute or post advertisements (including advertisements in the form of visual materials) or publicity materials related to motion pictures or to present them for public viewing through the information and communications networks shall in advance obtain a verification from the Korea Media Rating Board as to whether they are harmful to youth; provided, the same shall not apply to the advertising or publicity materials related to any restricted-rated motion pictures. <Amended on Jun. 5, 2008; May 8, 2009; Feb. 17, 2012>
  (2) Advertisement or publicity materials verified as harmful to youth under paragraph (1) shall not be distributed or posted; provided, the distribution or posting of advertising or publicity materials through information and communications networks shall be in accordance with Article 42-2 of the Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Network Utilization and Information Protection. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (3) Necessary matters concerning procedures and methods for verifying whether to be harmful to youth under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 33 (Limits on advertisement or promotion of restricted motion pictures) Any person who advertises or promotes any motion picture rated "Restricted" shall post or keep the advertising or promotion materials within restricted-screening theaters. In such cases, the relevant materials shall not be available outside the restricted-screening theaters. <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018>

SECTION 6 Korean Film Archive and Preservation of Motion Picture Films

Article 34 (Establishment of Korean Film Archive) (1) The Korean Film Archive shall be established under the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism to collect, preserve and display film materials including motion pictures and video products, documents related thereto, and sound materials, and to facilitate the artistic, historical and educational development of motion pictures and video products. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
  (2) The Korean Film Archive shall be a juristic person.
  (3) The Korean Film Archive shall have officers and employees as prescribed by its articles of association. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (4) The Korean Film Archive shall perform the following projects:
  1. The preservation of motion picture films, etc. submitted pursuant to Article 35 and compensation therefor;
  2. The collection of film materials including domestic and foreign motion pictures and video products, documents related thereto, and sound materials;
  3. The preservation and restoration of gathered film materials including motion pictures and video products, documents related thereto, and sound materials;
  4. The utilization and display of film materials including motion pictures and video products, documents related thereto, and sound materials to facilitate the development of the film and video culture;
  5. Projects for the informatization of film materials and the utilization of motion picture contents;
  6. Other projects necessary to accomplish the purposes of establishing the Korean Film Archive.
  (5) The provisions of the Civil Act concerning the incorporated foundation shall apply mutatis mutandis to the Korean Film Archive except as otherwise provided for in this Act. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (6) Expenses necessary to collect, preserve and utilize film materials as provided in paragraph (4) and to operate the Korean Film Archive may be provided from the National Treasury. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 35 (Submission of motion picture films) (1) Where motion picture makers have their motion pictures rated under Article 29(1), they shall submit the original films, disks, etc. of the relevant motion pictures or the copies and scripts thereof (hereinafter referred to as "motion picture films, etc.") to the Korean Film Archive, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) Where any person who imports or produces foreign motion pictures or any motion pictures referred to in Article 29(1) wants to preserve the relevant motion pictures, he or she may submit the relevant motion picture films, etc. to the Korean Film Archive. <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018>
  (3) The Korean Film Archive shall provide reasonable compensation to those who submit the motion picture films, etc. under paragraphs (1) and (2), as prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, financial resources necessary to compensate for films, etc. shall be borne by the National Treasury. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

SECTION 7 Screening of Motion Pictures

Article 36 (Registration of movie theaters) (1) Any person who intends to install and operate a movie theater shall get such movie theater registered with the head of a Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over a place where such movie theater is located after equipping the movie theater with facilities prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. The same shall also apply to cases where he or she alters registered matters. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 8, 2009>
  (2) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may designate and publish any place or establishment in which the installation of any restricted-screening theater is limited, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (3) The procedures for the registration of movie theaters and the alteration of such registration under paragraph (1), the delivery of registration certificates, and other matters necessary for registration shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 37 (Disaster prevention measures) (1) Any person who establishes and operates a movie theater after completing registration under Article 36(1) (hereinafter referred to as "movie theater operator") shall formulate a disaster plan, including preventive measures against fires or other disasters and plans for the relevant theater employees' roles and placement, etc., and shall report it annually to the head of the Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over the theater. In such cases, the head of the Si/Gun/Gu shall immediately notify the head of the competent fire station of the disaster plan as reported. <Amended on Jan. 26, 2007; Dec. 20, 2016; Oct. 16, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall review a disaster plan reported under the former part of paragraph (1) and accept the report if the plan is deemed appropriate. In such cases, where it is deemed that the details of the reported disaster plan are insufficient, any supplement may be requested. <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018>
  (3) A movie theater operator who is requested to supplement the disaster plan pursuant to the latter part of paragraph (2) shall supplement it according to the request unless there is good cause, and shall report it again to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. <Added on Dec. 24, 2018>
  (4) A movie theater operator shall take necessary disaster prevention measures in accordance with the disaster plan under the former part of paragraph (2) or paragraph (3). <Added on Dec. 20, 2016; Dec. 24, 2018>
  (5) Other matters necessary for disaster prevention measures for a movie theater shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Dec. 20, 2016; Dec. 24, 2018>

Article 38 (Support for exclusive movie theaters) (1) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may provide support to the movie theaters that screen any of the following motion pictures for not less than 60/100 of the annual running days (hereinafter referred to as "exclusive movie theaters") to protect youth viewers and promote the movie art: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Jan. 28, 2014; Mar. 13, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Korean movies;
  2. Animations, small films and short films, or art films or independent films recognized by the Korean Film Council;
  3. Motion pictures that persons under 18 years are permitted to view (referring to any motion pictures rated under Article 29(2)1 through 3).
  (2) In order to guarantee the rights of persons with disabilities to enjoy and benefit from culture, the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may provide support to exclusive movie theaters referred to in paragraph (1) that screen the motion pictures with Korean sign language, Korean subtitles, descriptive video services, etc. for not less than 30/100 of the annual running days. <Added on Jan. 28, 2014; Feb. 3, 2016>
  (3) Matters necessary for support for the exclusive movie theaters shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Jan. 28, 2014>

Article 38-2 (Improvement of environments to enjoy local motion pictures) (1) In order to improve the right to enjoy motion pictures in local areas, the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism and the Korean Film Council may provide support to local theaters, and assistance for the smooth distribution of motion pictures, public screening, construction of facilities for education in film and video culture, etc. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2016>
  (2) The Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism and the Korean Film Council may support projects and activities local residents who directly produce motion pictures or local organizations related to motion picture. <Added on Feb. 3, 2016>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 23, 2014]
  [Title Amended on Feb. 3, 2016]

Article 38-3 (Public movie theaters) (1) In order to promote the rights of local residents to enjoy watching motion pictures, the Government and local governments may establish or operate movie theaters for local residents.
  (2) Where the Government or a local government establishes or operates movie theaters pursuant to paragraph (1), it may entrust the duties, in whole or in part, to individuals or organizations.
  (3) The Government or a local government may give necessary support for the establishment or operation of the movie theaters under paragraph (1).
  [This Article Added on Mar. 13, 2018]

Article 38-4 (Rights of persons with disabilities to enjoy motion pictures) Motion picture enterprisers shall endeavor to provide closed films, Korean sign language interpretation, screen readers, etc. so that persons with disabilities can equally access and use the motion picture with persons without disabilities in order to improve their right to enjoy the motion picture.
  [This Article Added on, Dec. 28, 2021]

Article 39 (Integrated computer network for processing movie theater admission tickets) (1) The Korean Film Council shall operate an integrated computer network for processing movie theater admission tickets to ascertain the number of attending a movie theater and other matters relating to a movie theater promptly and accurately. <Amended on Mar. 17, 2010; May 18, 2015>
  (2) Each movie theater operator shall connect to the integrated computer network for handling movie theater admission thickets operated by the Korean Film Council. <Added on Mar. 17, 2010>
  (3) Each person who has joined the integrated computer network for handling movie theater admission tickets under paragraph (2) shall transmit the data related to the matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism, such as the number of audiences entering the relevant movie theater and the amount of sales of admission tickets, without intentional omission or manipulation. <Amended on May 18, 2015>
  (4) Matters necessary for operation, joining, etc. of the integrated computer network for handling movie theater admission tickets, in addition to the matters prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on May 18, 2015>
  [Title Amended on May 18, 2015]

Article 40 (Mandatory screening of Korean motion pictures) Operators of movie theaters shall screen Korean motion pictures for not less than the annual running days as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 41 (Reporting motion picture screening) (1) Where a person who manages a movie theater or who establishes and operates a non-permanent movie theater intends to screen a motion picture (excluding any motion picture provided for in Article 29(1); hereafter in this Article the same shall apply) or to change a motion picture on show into another one, he or she shall report to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu the matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism including the title, running period, etc. of the motion picture. The same shall also apply where he or she intends to suspend or resume the screening of the relevant motion picture or to reduce or extend the running period of the relevant motion picture. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Oct. 16, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) Notwithstanding the paragraph (1), a person who manages a movie theater that has joined the integrated computer network for handling movie theater admission thickets operated by the Korean Film Council under Article 39 is exempted from filing a report under paragraph (1). In such cases, the Korean Film Council shall notify the head of the relevant Si/Gun/Gu of the matters reported under paragraph (1). <Amended on Mar. 17, 2010; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (3) Matters necessary for the procedures, methods, etc. for reporting under paragraph (1) or (2) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 42 (Restrictions on motion picture screening) The head of Si/Gun/Gu shall prohibit or suspend the screening of the motion pictures falling under any one of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Motion pictures not rated for showing, in violation of Article 29(1) and (2);
  2. Motion pictures rated for showing by fraud or other improper means;
  3. Motion pictures shown by altering or violating their rating;
  4. Motion pictures shown by altering their contents differently from the rated ones;
  5. Other motion pictures not reported, in violation of Article 41.

Article 43 (Limits on screening and distribution of restricted motion pictures) (1) No one shall screen any restricted motion picture in a place or establishment that is not a restricted-screening theater.
  (2) No one shall make any motion picture that is the same as any restricted motion picture into any other visual product, including a video product, etc. or screen, sell, transmit or rent such produced visual product or offer it for the purpose of viewing.
  (3) Every restricted-screening theater shall be prohibited from screening the motion pictures specified in Article 29(2)1 through 4. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 44 (Qualified projectionists) The operators of movie theaters shall get any projectionists who have obtained the national technical qualifications for motion picture projection or has completed education in the field of motion picture projection under the conditions as prescribed by Presidential Decree to screen the relevant motion pictures; provided, the same shall not apply to the motion pictures, including small films, prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023; Dec. 31, 2024>
  [Amended provisions of Article 44 of the Act (Act No. 20622; Dec. 31, 2024) shall remain effective until December 31, 2027, in accordance with Article 3 of the Addenda of the same Act]

Article 45 (Suspension of business and cancellation of registration against movie theaters) (1) The head of Si/Gun/Gu may, in case that the operator of a movie theater falls under any one of the following subparagraphs, suspend his or her business for a fixed period of not more than 3 months or cancel the registration of the movie theater, as prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, in the cases of falling under subparagraph 1 or 8, the registration shall be canceled: <Amended on Dec. 20, 2016; Dec. 24, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Where he or she has got the movie theater registered by fraud or other improper;
  2. Where he or she has violated the provisions of Article 29(3) through (6);
  3. Where he or she has put up the advertising or publicity materials, or put them up in such a manner as to be seen from outside the restricted-screening theater, in violation of Article 33;
  4. Where he or she has failed to meet the standards for facilities referred to in Article 36(1);
  4-2. Where he or she has failed to take disaster prevention measures under Article 37(4);
  5. Where he or she has failed to screen Korean motion pictures for not less than the annual running days provided for in Article 40;
  6. Where he or she has failed to comply with the order to prohibit or suspend the screening of motion pictures under Article 42;
  7. Where he or she has been subjected to the business suspension on at least three occasions a year;
  8. Where he or she has run the business during the period of the business suspension.
  (2) If the operator of a movie theater violates the main clause of Article 29(4) or paragraph (5) of that Article, among the provisions of paragraph (1)2, in which cases it is recognized that the operator of a movie theater was unaware of the youth's age or that he or she was unable to verify the youth's age due to violence or intimidation, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may exempt him or her from the relevant administrative disposition, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Newly Inserted on Oct. 22, 2024>
  (3) When the head of a Si/Gun/Gu intends to cancel registration pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall hold a hearing. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023; Oct. 22, 2024>
  (4) The operator of a restricted-screening theater whose registration is canceled under paragraph (1) shall be prohibited from getting his or her restricted-screening theater registered at the same place within 3 years from the date of cancellation of his or her registration. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023; Oct. 22, 2024>

Article 46 (Succession of business) (1) Where a movie theater operator transfers his or her business, dies, or merges with other corporations, and where the transferee or successor, or a corporation established by the merger or the surviving corporation in a merger intends to succeed to the status of the previous movie theater operator, he or she shall report such fact to the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu within 30 days from the date of such transfer, succession, or merger, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism.
  (2) Where a person who has acquired a movie theater according to any of the following procedures intends to succeed to the status of the previous movie theater operator, he or she shall report such fact to the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu within 30 days from the date of such transfer, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism:
  1. Auction under the Civil Execution Act;
  2. Realization under the Debtor Rehabilitation and Bankruptcy Act;
  3. The sale of seized property under the National Tax Collection Act, the Customs Act, or the Local Tax Collection Act;
  4. Other procedures equivalent to those referred to in subparagraphs 1 through 3.
  (3) Upon receipt of a report under paragraph (1) or (2), the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall review the details thereof and accept the report if it conforms to this Act.
  (4) Where the report under paragraph (1) or (2) is accepted, the transferee, the successor, a corporation established by the merger or the surviving corporation in a merger, or a person who acquires the movie theater shall succeed to the status of the previous movie theater operator on the date of such transfer, succession, merger, or takeover.
  (5) Where the status of a movie theater operator is succeeded as a result of the acceptance of a report under paragraph (1), the effect of an administrative sanction that was imposed on the previous movie theater operator for a violation under the subparagraphs of Article 45(1) shall continue in force with the transferee, the successor, or the newly established or surviving corporation after merger for one year from the date on which the aforementioned sanction is taken; and where the procedures for an administrative sanction are under way, such procedures may be proceeded with against the transferee, the successor, or the newly established or surviving corporation after merger; provided, where the transferee, the successor, or the newly established or surviving corporation after merger was unaware of such sanction or violation at the time of acquisition of the business or the merger, the procedures for an administrative sanction shall not proceed.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 24, 2018]

Article 46-2 (Reporting on business closure of movie theaters and ex officio cancellation) (1) Where any person who has filed a report under Article 36 closes his or her business, he or she shall report on such business closure to the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu, within 30 days from the date of business closure, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Dec. 28, 2021>
  (2) Where any person subject to reporting on business closure under paragraph (1) fails to report thereon, the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu may ex officio cancel the registered matters after verifying his or her business closure, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

Article 47 (Support for overseeing activities by civic groups) (1) The Korean Film Council may provide necessary supports to civic groups, etc. which carry out the overseeing activities to protect the right and interest of moviegoers in connection with the screening of motion pictures, the operation of exclusive movie theaters, etc.
  (2) Such matters as may be necessary for the support, etc. under paragraph (1) shall be determined by the rules of the Korean Film Council. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

CHAPTER III VIDEO PRODUCTS

SECTION 1 Promotion of Video Industry

Article 48 (Framing and execution of policies for promotion of video industry) (1) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism shall frame and carry out such policies as may be necessary for the promotion of the video industry (hereinafter referred to as "policies for promotion"). <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
  (2) The policies for promotion shall include the matters set forth in the following subparagraphs with respect to video products: <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. The basic direction of the policies for promotion;
  2. Vitalization of creative activities;
  3. Promotion of export and creation of employment for the related industries;
  4. Development of technologies with respect to video products and improvement of levels thereof;
  5. Expansion of distribution facilities, specialization of distribution enterprises, and improvement of distribution structure;
  6. Acquisition and operation of financial resources for the promotion of the video industry;
  7. Training of experts;
  8. Construction of infrastructure for video products-related industries and the creation and operation of the relevant industrial clusters;
  9. Regulation and control of any video products that are manufactured, imported, distributed, sold, rented, or offered for viewing in violation of this Act (hereinafter referred to as "illegal video products");
  10. Assistance to nonprofit private organizations under Article 2 of the Assistance for Nonprofit Non-Governmental Organizations Act (hereinafter referred to as "nonprofit private organizations") that carry out voluntary overseeing activities to monitor illegal video products;
  11. Other matters relating to the development of the video industry.

Article 49 (Establishment and operation of Video Industry Promotion Committee) (1) The Video Industry Promotion Committee shall be established in the Korean Film Council in order to carry out policies for promotion of the video industry in an efficient manner.
  (2) The Video Industry Promotion Committee shall consist of not more than seven members including its chairperson.
  (3) The chairperson of the Video Industry Promotion Committee shall be elected by mutual vote among its members, and the members of the Video Industry Promotion Committee shall be commissioned by the chairperson of the Korean Film Council from among the members of the Korean Film Council and the persons with ample expertise and experiences in the field of the video industry. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (4) Such matters as may be necessary for the composition, operation, etc. of the Video Industry Promotion Committee under paragraph (1) shall be determined by the rules of the Korean Film Council. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 49-2 (Rights of persons with disabilities to enjoy video products) Video product business operators shall endeavor to provide closed captioning, Korean sign language interpretation, screen readers, etc. so that persons with disabilities can equally access and use the video products to improve their enjoyment of video products.
  [This Article Added on, Dec. 28, 2021]

SECTION 2 Rating

Article 50 (Rating) (1) A person who manufactures or distributes (including imports; hereinafter the same shall apply) video products shall have the contents of the relevant video products rated by the Korea Media Rating Board before he or she supplies the relevant products; provided, the same shall not apply to any of the following video products: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Feb. 17, 2012; Oct. 16, 2018; Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Video products offered free of charge for viewing by specific persons not including youth at a specific place;
  2. Online video products offered free of charge for public viewing through information and communications networks; provided, this shall not apply to music and video files a person who operates business referred to in subparagraphs 8 through 11 of Article 2 of the Music Industry Promotion Act has manufactured, distributed or provided for public viewing;
  3. Video products offered for viewing at any media contest or exhibition, etc. recommended by the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism or the head of the relevant central administrative agency;
  4. Video products of the same contents as the motion pictures which have already been rated (excluding any restricted motion pictures). In such cases, the rating of the relevant motion pictures shall be deemed as that of the video products;
  5. Video products prescribed by Presidential Decree that the rating is unnecessary in the light of their producers, types of distribution, etc.
  (2) The Korea Media Rating Board shall confirm that a person who applies for the rating of video products pursuant to paragraph (1) has a legitimate right to produce or distribute the video products; provided, the same shall not apply where matters concerning legitimate rights for video products produced or imported for the purpose of providing them to a city hall using information and communication networks are displayed in an electronic manner prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Sep. 27, 2022>
  (3) The rating of video products under paragraph (1) shall be as follows, based on the contents of the video products and the degree of expression of image, scripts, etc.: <Amended on May 8, 2009; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. All ages admitted: Video products that all ages are permitted to watch;
  2. 12 years or older admitted: Video products that persons aged 12 or older are permitted to watch;
  3. 15 years or older admitted: Video products that persons aged 15 or older are permitted to watch;
  4. Adults only: Video products that the youth are not permitted to watch;
  5. Restricted: Video products that need to be restricted to a certain extent in offering for its watching, distribution, etc. as they excessively express lewdness, violence, social acts, etc., and thus are likely to markedly hamper the universal human dignity, social values, good morals, or national sentiment.
  (4) Deleted. <May 8, 2009>
  (5) Detailed criteria for the rating under paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree based on the matters referred to in Article 29(7). <Amended on May 8, 2009>
  (6) The Korea Media Rating Board shall issue the following documents to the applicant when it has rated a video product: <Amended on May 8, 2009; Oct. 16, 2018>
  1. A video rating certificate stating the rating and content information of the video product;
  2. Documents stating duties to be fulfilled according to the rating.
  (7) Procedures and methods for the rating, procedures for issuing a video rating certificate, and other necessary matters under paragraphs (1), (2) and (6) shall be determined by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Added on May 8, 2009; Oct. 16, 2018>

Article 50-2 (Rating by independent rating business entity) (1) Notwithstanding Article 50(1), an independent rating business entity designated under Article 50-3(1) (hereinafter referred to as "independent rating business entity") may perform the internal rating of the contents of online video products the price for which is offered by their users before providing the relevant online video products; provided, online video products falling under Article 50(3)5 shall be rated by the Korea Media Rating Board.
  (2) An independent rating business entity shall verify that a person who supplies online video products, the rating of which is conducted by himself or herself under paragraph (1), has the legitimate right to manufacture or distribute such video products; provided, the same shall not apply where the relevant person has indicated matters concerning a legitimate right by an electronic method prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) In cases where an independent rating business entity deems it difficult to conduct independent rating, he or she may request the Korea Media Rating Board to grant rating, which may substitute for an independent rating.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 27, 2022]

Article 50-3 (Designation and re-designation of independent rating business entity) (1) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may designate an independent rating business entity allowed to conduct independent rating for a specified period not exceeding five years after examining the following matters among the business entities fulfilling the requirements under paragraph (2):
  1. Propriety of the plan for operating independent rating affairs;
  2. Propriety of the plan for protecting youths and users.
  (2) Business entities shall meet the following requirements to be designated pursuant to paragraph (1):
  1. A business entity falling under any of the following:
  a. A corporation that engages in business providing online video products for viewing and files a report on value-added telecommunications business prescribed in Article 22 of the Telecommunications Business Act;
  b. A CATV broadcasting business entity or satellite broadcasting business entity under the Broadcasting Act or an Internet multimedia broadcast service providers pursuant to the Internet Multimedia Broadcast Services Act;
  2. No record of revocation of designation or suspension of business under Article 50-6(1) for the latest five years.
  (3) Where an independent rating business entity intends to continue to conduct the affairs of independent rating after the expiration of the designation period under paragraph (1), he or she shall obtain re-designation from the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism.
  (4) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may conduct re-designation under paragraph (3), the period of which shall not exceed five years, after deliberating on the following:
  1. Whether the plans referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) have been implemented and the propriety of the future plan;
  2. No record of finalized business suspension under Article 50-6(1) during the latest five years;
  3. Whether the results of the evaluation and improvement measures under Article 50-8(4) have been implemented.
  (5) Matters necessary for the evaluation and designation under paragraph (1) and the detailed standards, procedures, etc. for re-designation under paragraph (4) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 27, 2022]

Article 50-4 (Matters to be observed by independent rating business entities) Independent rating business entities shall comply with the following:
  1. The relevant video product shall be rated by itself in accordance with the standards for rating of video products under Article 50(5);
  2. The rating, content information, etc. of the online video products rated pursuant to subparagraph 1 shall be indicated pursuant to Article 65 and notified to the Korea Media Rating Board;
  3. A person responsible for rating shall be designated, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism, and the relevant person responsible for rating shall receive education necessary for the affairs of rating conducted by the Korea Media Rating Board;
  4. The entity shall comply with the request for submission of data by the Korea Media Rating Board related to duties of independent rating;
  5. The entity shall comply with other matters necessary for maintaining the appropriateness of independent rating affairs, which are prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 27, 2022]

Article 50-5 (Effects of independent rating) (1) The online video products rated by an independent rating business entity pursuant to Article 50-2(1) shall be deemed rated by the Korea Media Rating Board pursuant to Article 50(1) within the scope supplied and provided by the independent rating business entity.
  (2) An independent rating business entity may use the result of rating conducted by another independent rating business entity.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 27, 2022]

Article 50-6 (Revocation of designation of independent rating business entity) (1) Where an independent rating business entity falls under any of the following, the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may revoke the designation thereof or order a suspension of business under the main clause of Article 50-2(1) for a specified period not exceeding six months; provided, the Minister shall revoke the designation in cases falling under subparagraph 1:
  1. Where the entity has been designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where the entity has failed to observe the matters under Article 50-4;
  3. Where the entity has failed to take measures under Article 50-7(4);
  4. Where the entity has continued its business in violation of the business suspension order.
  (2) Where any of the grounds referred to in paragraph (1)2 arises, the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may determine corrective measures and recommend that an independent rating business entity accept such measures within ten days prior to the revocation of designation or an order to suspend business, and where an independent rating business entity takes corrective measures, the Minister need not revoke the designation or order to suspend business under paragraph (1).
  (3) Where the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism revokes the designation or orders suspension of business operations pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister shall hold a hearing under the Administrative Procedures Act.
  (4) The revocation of designation pursuant to paragraph (1) shall not have an influence on the effect of rating conducted by an independent rating business entity before such revocation; provided, this shall not apply to the revocation of designation on the grounds referred to in paragraph (1)1.
  (5) Procedures necessary for the revocation of designation and an order for suspension of business under paragraph (1) and detailed standards for application shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 27, 2022]

Article 50-7 (Ex officio re-rating) (1) Where the Korea Media Rating Board deems that an online video product rated by an independent rating business entity falls under Article 50(3)4 or 5, it may conduct re-rating or revoke a decision on rating by an independent rating business entity, at the request of the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism or ex officio.
  (2) Where the result of rating seriously violates the rating standards, or where the results of rating conducted by independent rating business entities are different from each other, the Korea Media Rating Board may request independent rating business entities to adjust ratings.
  (3) Where the Korea Media Rating Board makes a decision on rating or a decision on revocation thereof under paragraph (1), it shall notify an independent rating business entity of such decision without delay.
  (4) Upon receipt of a request under paragraph (2) or a notification under paragraph (3), the independent rating business entity shall take measures accordingly without delay.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 27, 2022]

Article 50-8 (Support activities of the Korea Media Rating Board) (1) The Korea Media Rating Board may conduct the following to support the efficient performance of the duties of independent rating:
  1. Establishment and provision of a database of rating information of online video products;
  2. Establishment and operation of information and communications systems for the procedures for prompt rating of online video products;
  3. Establishment and operation of on-line post management systems to verify compliance with subparagraphs 1 and 2 of Article 50-4;
  4. Establishment and operation of online educational systems and face-to-face educational systems for persons in charge of rating under subparagraph 3 of Article 50-4;
  5. Support for the affairs related to rating for the internal rating business entities of small enterprises under Article 2(2) of the Framework Act on Small and Medium Enterprises;
  6. Establishment and operation of on-line educational systems and face-to-face educational systems to improve the capacity of users of information on independent rating;
  7. Improvement of systems and establishment of cooperation systems for the protection of the rights and interests of users of independent rating information.
  (2) The Korea Media Rating Board may form and operate its own sectional committees for the purpose of effectively carrying out the activities prescribed in each subparagraph of paragraph (1), as prescribed by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board.
  (3) The Korea Media Rating Board may disclose the matters notified by independent rating business entities to the public pursuant to subparagraph 2 of Article 50-4.
  (4) The Korea Media Rating Board shall assess compliance with the matters prescribed in Article 50-4 and the duty to take measures under Article 50-7(4) every year to maintain the appropriateness of the duties of independent rating, notify the independent rating business entity of the assessment results, and may recommend that the business entity improve the relevant affairs depending on such results.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 27, 2022]

Article 51 (Confirmation of reproduction) (1) Any person who intends to reproduce the rated video products into any other video products whose contents are identical to those of the said video products or to distribute such reproduced video products (hereafter in this Article referred to as "producer, etc.") shall file an application with the Korea Media Rating Board, accompanied by documents attesting his or her legitimate right to reproduce or distribute such video products to confirm as to whether the contents of the reproduced video products are identical to those of the original video products; provided, the same shall not apply where the Korea Media Rating Board confirms that the producer, etc. holds the right to reproduce the rated video products into any other video products whose contents are identical to those of the said video products or to distribute such reproduced video products when he or she files an application for their rating under Article 50(1). <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) Where the Korea Media Rating Board confirms that the producer, etc. is the legitimate right holder and the contents of the reproduced video products are identical to those of the original video products pursuant to paragraph (1), it shall issue a certificate of confirmation to the producer, etc. <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018>
  (3) Necessary matters concerning procedures for the confirmation, the issuance and re-issuance of a certificate of confirmation, etc. under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 52 (Revocation of rating) (1) Where the rating under Article 50(1) or 50-2(3) or the confirmation under Article 51(1) has been granted by any false or other unjust means or to a person without any legitimate right, the Korea Media Rating Board shall revoke the relevant rating or confirmation. <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018; Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) Where the Korea Media Rating Board has revoked the rating or confirmation under paragraph (1), the person subject to a disposition of revocation shall return to the Korea Media Rating Board the rating certificate under Article 50(5) or the certificate of confirmation under Article 51(2) within seven days from the date when he or she is notified of such disposition. <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 53 (Prohibition of sales of illegal video products) (1) No person shall manufacture, supply, sell, rent (hereinafter referred to as "distribution"), offer to the public for viewing, display, or keep any of the following video products: <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018; Sep. 27, 2022>
  1. The video products that have not yet been rated, in violation of Article 50(1) or 50-2(1);
  2. The video products that have been reproduced or distributed without obtaining confirmation, in violation of Article 51(1);
  3. The relevant video products for which the rating or confirmation has been revoked pursuant to Article 50-7(1) or 52(1);
  4. The video products that have been manufactured, imported or distributed by the person who has failed to file a report thereon, in violation of Article 57(1);
  5. The video products the contents of which have been altered differently from the rated ones or the rating of which has been altered.
  (2) No person shall offer the public the rated video products for viewing, in violation of the rating prescribed in Article 50(3); provided, in the case of an independent rating business entity, this shall not apply where a legal representative, such as a person with parental authority, etc., provides a means to provide guidance to watch, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (3) Article 30 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the confirmation of age under paragraph (2). <Added on Oct. 22, 2024>
  (4) The rating certificate and the certificate of confirmation issued under Articles 50(5) and 51(2) shall not be sold or donated, except for business succession under Article 63. <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023; Oct. 22, 2024>

Article 53-2 (Restrictions on offering for viewing and distribution of video products with restricted rating) (1) No person may offer a video product with a restricted rating for viewing at a place or facilities, other than a video-viewing mini-theater for video products with a restricted rating.
  (2) No person may distribute a video product with a restricted rating.
  (3) No video-viewing mini-theater for video products with a restricted rating may offer a video product under Article 50(3)1 through 4 to the general public for their viewing.
  [This Article Added on May 8, 2009]

Article 54 (Reclassification of rating) (1) Any person dissatisfied with a decision on classifications under Article 50 or 50-2(3) may raise an objection to the Korea Media Rating Board to review the rating, specifying the reasons therefor, within 30 days from the date he or she is notified of the decision. <Amended on Feb. 17, 2012; Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) The Korea Media Rating Board shall examine an objection raised under paragraph (1), and if the objection is well-grounded, shall notify the applicant or his or her agent of the rating reclassified within 15 days from the date of receipt of the objection; if the objection is not well-grounded, shall notify the applicant or his or her agent thereof. In such cases, the period for notification shall not be extended. <Amended on May. 16, 2023>
  (3) Matters necessary for the procedures and methods for filing an objection under paragraphs (1) and (2), the notification thereof, etc. shall be prescribed by the Regulations of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Amended on May. 16, 2023>
  [Title Amended on May 16, 2023]

Article 55 (Notification of rating) When the Korea Media Rating Board has made a decision falling under any one of the following subparagraphs, it shall give a written notice of the decision to the heads of the administrative agencies prescribed by Presidential Decree that are vested with the authority of guidance and control over video products and to the video product organization under Article 87 (hereinafter referred to as the "video product organization"), and also publicly announce it through an information communications network: <Amended on May 8, 2009; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Decision on the rating under Article 50(1) and (3);
  2. Decision on an objection filed under Article 54.

Article 56 (Request for submission of materials) The Korea Media Rating Board may, if necessary for carrying out the business affairs as prescribed in Articles 50, 50-2(3), and 54, request persons who applies for rating, etc. to submit necessary materials related to the examination of such rating. <Amended on Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023>

SECTION 3 Report, Registration and Operation of Business

Article 57 (Report on video product manufacturing business) (1) A person who intends to carry on a video product manufacturing business or a video product distributing business shall report thereon to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu; provided, in cases falling under any one of the following subparagraphs, such business may be operated without filing a report thereon: <Amended on May 8, 2009; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Where such video products are manufactured by the State or local governments;
  2. Where such video products are manufactured by educational or training institutions established by Acts and subordinate statutes in order to be used for their internal education or training;
  3. Where such video products are manufactured by broadcasting business operators under the Broadcasting Act in order to be used for the broadcast purpose;
  4. Where such video products are manufactured by public institutions under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions in order to be used for the publicity of their business;
  5. Where such video products are manufactured for the purpose of preserving ceremonial occasions or religious rituals in memory; provided, the same shall not include the cases of distributing or offering them to the public for the purpose of their viewing;
  6. Where such video products are manufactured and distributed for the purpose of offering them to the public for their viewing only through information and communications networks;
  7. Where such video products are manufactured for any other purposes than to distribute or offer them to many and unspecified persons for the purpose of their viewing;
  8. Where the video products falling under any subparagraph of Article 50(1) are manufactured.
  (2) Deleted. <Mar. 25, 2025>
  (3) Matters necessary for the procedures, methods, etc. for reporting under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 24, 2018; Mar. 25, 2025>

Article 58 (Registration of video service providing business) (1) A person who intends to run video service providing business shall register his or her business with the head of a Si/Gun/Gu after being equipped with facilities prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism; provided, such business may be operated without the registration thereof, in any of the following cases: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Where such business falls under an Internet computer game facility providing business under the Game Industry Promotion Act;
  2. Where such business is engaged in offering video products to the public for their viewing only through information and communications networks.
  (2) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, designate and publicly announce areas or facilities in which a video-viewing mini-theater for video products with a restricted rating may not be established. <Added on May 8, 2009>
  (3) Necessary matters for the procedures, methods, etc. of registration referred to in paragraph (1) shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 8, 2009; Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 59 (Restriction on business) Where any person who intends to file a report or registration under Article 57 or 58 falls under any one of the following subparagraphs, he or she may not report or register his or her business under Article 57 or 58: <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Where a person (including, in the case of a corporation, its representative or officers), for whom a year has not passed yet since he or she was subjected to an order given to close his or her business or a disposition to revoke its registration, or for whom the term of business suspension has not expired yet since he or she was subjected to the disposition of business suspension, under Article 67(1) or (2), intends to operate the same type of business again;
  2. Where a person for whom a year has not passed yet since he or she was subjected to an order given to close his or her business or a disposition to revoke its registration, or for whom the term of business suspension has not expired yet since he or she was subjected to the disposition of business suspension, under Article 67(1) or (2), intends to operate the same type of business at the same place (excluding a video product manufacturing business).

Article 60 (Issuance of certificate of report or registration) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall, upon receiving a report under Article 57 or completing registration under Article 58, issue the certificate of report or the certificate of registration to the applicant, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 8, 2009; Dec. 24, 2018; Mar. 25, 2025>

Article 61 (Alterations of reported or registered matters) (1) Where a person who has filed a report or filed for registration under Article 57 or 58 intends to alter such material matters as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism, he or she shall file a report on or file for registration of alterations with the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 8, 2009>
  (2) Deleted. <Mar. 25, 2025>
  (3) Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu receives a report of modifications or completes the registration of modifications under paragraph (1), he or she shall renew and issue the certificate of registration or the certificate of report, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May. 8, 2009; Dec. 24, 2018; Mar. 25, 2025>
  (4) Matters necessary for the procedures and methods for reporting and registration of modifications under paragraphs (1) and (3) and for the renewal, etc. of the certificate of report and the certificate of registration shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Dec. 24, 2018; Mar. 25, 2025>

Article 62 (Matters to be observed by video service providing business operator) (1) A person who carries on a video service providing business shall observe the following matters: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 8, 2009; Feb. 17, 2012; Aug. 8, 2023; Oct. 22, 2024>
  1. To take measures for the prevention of fires or accidents within the place of business;
  2. In cases of the business running a video-viewing mini-theater, to admit youth thereinto only during the admission hours prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, the same shall not apply to cases where youth are accompanied by their parent or other guardians or carry a written consent to admission given by their parent or other guardians, or other cases prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  3. In cases of the business of running a video-viewing establishment and the business of providing multiple types of visual materials, not to conduct the following acts:
  a. Selling or offering alcohol;
  b. Employing a service man or woman or assisting any other person to employ such person;
  c. Deleted; <May 8, 2009>
  4. In cases of the business of running a video-viewing establishment, the business of running a video-viewing mini-theater for video products with a restricted rating, and the business of providing multiple types of visual materials, to prohibit youth from admitting thereinto after confirming their age;
  5. To post the certificate of registration at the place of business, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism.
  (2) Article 30 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the confirmation of age under paragraph (1)2 and 4. <Added on Oct. 22, 2024>

Article 63 (Succession of business) (1) Where a video product manufacturing business operator, a video product distribution business operator, or a video service providing business operator who files a report or files for registration pursuant to Article 57 or 58 (hereinafter referred to as "video product manufacturing business operator, etc.") transfers his or her business, dies, or merges with other corporations, and where the transferee or successor, or a corporation established by the merger or the surviving corporation in a merger intends to succeed to the status of the previous video product manufacturing business operator, etc., he or she shall report such fact to the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu within 30 days from the date of such transfer, succession, or merger, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism.
  (2) Where a person who has acquired all of the business facilities and apparatuses related to video products of a video product manufacturing business operator, etc. (referring to the major facilities and apparatuses prescribed by Presidential Decree) according to any of the following procedures intends to succeed to the status of the previous video product manufacturing business operator, etc., he or she shall report such fact to the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu within 30 days from the date of such transfer, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism:
  1. Auction under the Civil Execution Act;
  2. Realization under the Debtor Rehabilitation and Bankruptcy Act;
  3. The sale of seized property under the National Tax Collection Act, the Customs Act, or the Local Tax Collection Act;
  4. Other procedures equivalent to those referred to in subparagraphs 1 through 3.
  (3) Upon receipt of a report filed under paragraph (1) or (2) with respect to a video service providing business operator registered under Article 58, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall review the details of the report and accept it if it complies with this Act. <Amended on Mar. 25, 2025>
  (4) Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu receives a report filed by a video product manufacturing business operator or a video product distributing business operator, or accepts a report filed by a video product service providing business operator under paragraph (1) or (2), the transferee, successor, a corporation established by the merger or surviving the merger, or a person who takes over all of the business facilities or apparatus related to video products shall succeed to the status of the previous video product manufacturing business operator, etc. on the date of transfer, inheritance, merger, or takeover. <Amended on Mar. 25, 2025>
  (5) Where a video product manufacturing business operator, etc. whose business was closed or whose registration was revoked as a result of a report on business closure filed under Article 64 files a report on or files for registration of the same type of business again at the same place within one year, he or she shall succeed to the status of the video product manufacturing business operator, etc. before filing a report on business closure.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 24, 2018]

Article 64 (Reporting on business closure of video product manufacturing business, video product distribution business or video service providing business, and ex officio cancellation) (1) When a business operator who has filed a report under Article 57 or registration under Article 58 closes his or her business, he or she shall make a report on the business closure to the head of a relevant Si/Gun/Gu within 30 days from the date of business closure, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 8, 2009; May 18, 2015; Dec. 28, 2021; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) Where a business operator subject to reporting on business closure under paragraph (1) fails to file a report on the business closure, the head of a relevant Si/Gun/Gu may ex officio cancel the matters reported or registered after confirming the fact of his or her business closure, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 8, 2009; May 18, 2015>
  [Title Amended on May 18, 2015]

SECTION 4 Indication on and Advertisement of Video Products

Article 65 (Obligation of indication) (1) A person who commercially manufactures, imports, or reproduces video products shall indicate the trade name of the manufacturer, importer or reproducer (referring to a trade name of a publishing company in the case of video products attached to publications), the rating under Article 50(1) or 50-2(1), content information, and other matters determined by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism, on each of the relevant video products. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 8, 2009; Oct. 16, 2018; Sep. 27, 2022>
  (2) Necessary matters pertaining to the methods of indicating the rating and content information under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on May 8, 2009; Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 66 (Restrictions on advertisement and publicity) (1) A person who intends to distribute or post the advertising or publicity materials related to video products (excluding the video products referred to in Article 50(3)1 through 3) or to offer them for public viewing through the information and communications networks shall obtain a verification from the Korea Media Rating Board as to whether they are harmful to youth; provided, the same shall not apply to the advertising or publicity materials of a video product with a restricted rating. <Amended on May 8, 2009; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) Advertising or publicity materials verified as harmful to youth under paragraph (1) shall not be distributed or posted. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (3) Notwithstanding the provisions of paragraph (2), any advertising or publicity materials through information and communications networks may be offered to the general public excluding youth for the purpose of their viewing in accordance with Article 42-2 of the Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Network Utilization and Information Protection; provided, the same shall not apply where the contents of such advertising or publicity materials are verified by the Korea Media Rating Board as falling under any subparagraph of Article 50(3)5. <Amended on May 8, 2009; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (4) No person shall distribute or post advertising and publicity materials showing any contents or rating that differs from the original contents or rating of a classified video product.
  (5) A person who advertises or publicizes a video product with a restricted rating shall post such advertising or publicity materials in a video-viewing mini-theater for video products with a restricted rating. In such cases, the relevant materials posted shall not be visible from outside a video-viewing mini-theater for video products with a restricted rating. <Added on May 8, 2009>
  (6) Necessary matters concerning procedures for verification as to whether to be harmful to youth under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Amended on May 8, 2009; Aug. 8, 2023>

SECTION 5 Revocation of Registration and Other Administrative Measures

Article 67 (Administrative disposition) (1) In case where any person who has filed a report on a video product manufacturing business or a video product distribution business falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may order the suspension of the business for a fixed period not exceeding 6 months or the closure of the business; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 4, he or she shall order the closure of the business: <Amended on May 8, 2009; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Where he or she has filed the report by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where he or she has violated the provisions of Article 53(1);
  3. Where he or she has failed to file a report of alteration as prescribed in Article 61(1);
  4. Where he or she has continued to run his or her business in violation of the order given to suspend his or her business.
  (2) In case any person who conducts a video service providing business falls under any one of the following subparagraphs, the head of Si/Gun/Gu may order the business to be suspended for a fixed period not exceeding six months or revoke the registration; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1 or 7, he or she shall revoke the registration: <Amended on May 8, 2009; Aug. 8, 2023; Oct. 22, 2024>
  1. When he or she fails for registration by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where he or she has violated the provisions of Article 53(1) or (2);
  3. Where he or she has failed to meet the standards for facilities as referred to in Article 58(1);
  4. Where he or she has failed to file a report of alteration as prescribed in Article 61(1);
  5. Where he or she has failed to abide by the matters to be observed as referred to in Article 62(1);
  6. Where he or she conducts sexual traffic, etc., or arranges or provides sexual traffic, etc., referred to in Article 2(1) of the Act on the Punishment of Arrangement of Commercial Sex Acts;
  7. Where he or she has continued to run his or her business in violation of the order given to suspend his or her business.
  (3) If the operator of a movie theater violates the main clause of Article 29(4) or paragraph (5) of that Article, among the provisions of paragraph (1)2, in which cases it is recognized that the operator of a movie theater was unaware of the youth's age or that he or she was unable to verify the youth's age due to violence or intimidation, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may exempt him or her from the relevant administrative disposition, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Newly Inserted on Oct. 22, 2024>
  (4) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall hold a hearing if he or she intends to order the closure of business or revoke the registration in accordance with paragraphs (1) and (2). <Amended on Oct. 22, 2024>
  (5) A person who has received an order to close his or her business or a disposition to revoke registration under paragraph (1) or (2) shall return the certificate of report or the certificate of registration within 7 days from the date he or she is notified of such disposition. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023; Oct. 22, 2024>
  (6) The detailed standards for administrative dispositions under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism, taking into account the type of violation, the degree of violation, etc. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 8, 2023; Oct. 22, 2024>

Article 68 (Imposition of penalty surcharge) (1) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may, when a video product manufacturer or video product distributor fails to file a report on alteration, as prescribed in Article 61(1), impose a penalty surcharge not exceeding 30 million won in lieu of the disposition of suspension of business, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Dec. 28, 2021>
  (2) Where the head of Si/Gun/Gu is to take a disposition to suspend the business of a video service providing business operator because he or she falls under any one of the following, he or she may, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, impose on such business operator a penalty surcharge not exceeding 30 million won in lieu of the disposition to suspend the business: <Amended on Dec. 28, 2021; Aug. 8, 2023; Oct. 22, 2024>
  1. Where he or she has failed to meet the standards for facilities as referred to in Article 58(1);
  2. Where he or she violates the matters to be observed in accordance with Article 62(1)2, 4, or 5.
  (3) Where a person who is obligated to pay the penalty surcharge pursuant to paragraph (1) or (2) fails to pay it within the time limit for payment, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall collect it in accordance with the Act on the Collection of Local Administrative Penalty Charges. <Amended on Aug. 6, 2013; Mar. 24, 2020; Dec. 28, 2021>
  (4) The sum of money that is collected as penalty surcharge pursuant to paragraphs (1) through (3) shall be spent for the following purposes: <Amended on Dec. 28, 2021>
  1. The manufacturing and distribution of wholesome video products;
  2. The improvement of harmful environment proper to video products.
  (5) Necessary matters pertaining to the sum of penalty surcharge set according to types, degrees, etc. of violations liable to penalty surcharge under paragraph (1) or (2), the operational plan for penalty surcharge under paragraph (4), etc. shall be determined by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Dec. 28, 2021>

Article 69 (Succession to effect of administrative sanction disposition) (1) Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu receives a report from a video production business operator or a video distribution business operator, or accepts a report from a video service provider under Article 63(1), thereby allowing succession to the status of such operator, the effects of any administrative sanctions imposed on the former operator under any subparagraph of Article 67(1) or (2) shall be succeeded by the transferee, heir, or a corporation newly established or surviving after a merger for a period of 1 year from the date of the administrative disposition, and if the administrative sanction procedure is still pending, such procedure may be continued against the transferee, heir, or the newly established or surviving corporation; provided, if the transferee, heir, or the newly established or surviving corporation was not aware of the disposition or the relevant violation at the time of the transfer or merger, the administrative sanction procedure shall not be continued. <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018; Mar. 25, 2025>
  (2) Where the status of a video product manufacturing business operator, etc. is succeeded pursuant to Article 63(5), the effect of an administrative sanction that was imposed for a violation of any subparagraph of Article 67(1) or (2) before the report of business closure is filed shall continue in force with the person who succeeds to the status of the video product manufacturing business operator, etc. for one year from the date on which the aforementioned sanction expires; and where the procedures for an administrative sanction are under way, such procedures may be proceeded with against the person who succeeds to the status of the video product manufacturing business operator, etc. <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018>

Article 70 (Closure and removal) (1) When any person operates business without reporting or registration under Articles 57 and 58, or when any person continues business after he or she was ordered to close the business or was subject to a disposition of revocation of registration in accordance with Article 67(1) or (2), the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may direct the relevant public officials to take the following actions, in order to close the relevant place of business: <Amended on May 8, 2009; Oct. 16, 2018>
  1. To eliminate signboards or any other signs of business on the relevant business or the relevant place of business;
  2. To post a notice informing that the relevant business or the relevant place of business is unlawful;
  3. To affix seals to such apparatuses or facilities as may be necessary for operation of the business in order to prevent them from being used.
  (2) The relevant video product business operator or his or her agent shall be notified in writing before the actions under paragraph (1) are taken; provided, this shall not apply if an urgent action is required for the safety and welfare of the general public. <Amended on Mar. 28, 2008; Oct. 16, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (3) When any video products referred to in Article 53(1) are found, the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism, the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or a Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as "Mayor/Do Governor"), or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may direct the relevant public officials to remove and destroy them. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; May 8, 2009; May 18, 2015>
  (4) When a public official has removed the relevant video products pursuant to paragraph (3), he or she shall issue a certificate of removal to the owner or possessor of such products; provided, the same shall not apply where he or she refused to receive it. <Amended on Oct. 16, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (5) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may, if necessary, request the video product organization to cooperate with the relevant public officials in performing the duties of removal and destruction pursuant to paragraph (3). <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (6) Except as otherwise provided in paragraphs (1) through (5), Article 32 or 33 of the Framework Act on Administration shall apply to administrative compulsion under paragraphs (1) and (3). <Amended on Mar. 25, 2025>
  (7) Where an online video product falls under any subparagraph of Article 53 (1), the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may request that the Korea Media and Communications Commission issue a corrective order to the information and communications service provider under Article 2(1)3 of the Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Network Utilization and Information Protection or to a person who manages and operates the bulletin board under Article 2(1)9 to take correctional measures such as refusal, suspension, or restriction of such handling. In such cases, prior deliberation by the Korea Media Rating Board shall be required. <Added on Sep. 27, 2022; Oct. 1, 2025>
  (8) The Korea Media Rating Board may, after deliberation pursuant to the latter part of paragraph (7), request that the Korea Media and Communications Standards Commission under Article 18 of the Act on the Establishment and Operation of Korea Media and Communications Commission deliberate and make a request for correction pursuant to subparagraph 4 of Article 22 of that Act. <Added on Sep. 27, 2022; Oct. 1, 2025>

CHAPTER IV KOREA MEDIA RATING BOARD

Article 71 (Korea Media Rating Board) The Korea Media Rating Board shall be established to secure the ethical and public responsibilities of motion pictures, video products, and other materials for their advertisement and publicity (hereinafter referred to as "visual representations, etc.") and to thereby protect youth.

Article 72 (Duties) The Korea Media Rating Board shall deliberate and decide upon the following matters: <Amended on May 8, 2009; Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Matters concerning the rating, content information of visual representations, etc. and ascertainment as to whether they are harmful to youth;
  2. Matters concerning the fact-finding surveys on the manufacturing, distribution, and offering of visual representations, etc. for viewing, and concerning other control thereof;
  2-2. Matters concerning the post management of independent rating, such as verification as to whether subparagraph 1 or 2 of Article 50-4 is complied with;
  3. Matters concerning the establishment, amendment and repeal of the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board;
  4. Matters concerning the application for challenge of members under Article 74(2);
  5. Matters concerning the research, study, international cooperation, and education and public relations for the purpose of securing the objectivity of the rating of visual representations, etc.;
  5-2. Matters concerning the education for persons responsible for rating and users of online video products;
  5-3. Matters concerning the protection of rights and interests of users of online video products;
  6. Such other matters as determined or entrusted by this Act or other Acts and subordinate statutes as the duties or authority of the Korea Media Rating Board.

Article 73 (Composition) (1) The Korea Media Rating Board shall consist of not more than nine members including a chairperson and a vice chairperson.
  (2) Members of the Korea Media Rating Board shall be commissioned by the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism from among those who are engaged in the fields of culture and arts, visual materials, etc., youth, law, education, journalism, or non-profit, non-governmental organizations, etc. and have expertise and experience, who are recommended by the heads of the organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on May 8, 2009; Aug. 8, 2023; Jan. 31, 2025>
  (3) The Korea Media Rating Board shall be composed of members in consideration of the following matters: <Amended on Jan. 31, 2025>
  1. Appropriate distribution across the recommended fields;
  2. Expertise and work experience in the relevant field;
  3. Balance of sex and age. In such cases, a specific gender shall not exceed two-thirds of the total number of members.
  (4) Other necessary matters concerning the formation and operation of the Korea Media Rating Board shall be determined by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Added on May 8, 2009>

Article 74 (Exclusion, challenge, and refrainment of members) (1) With respect to any case falling under any one of the following subparagraphs, the relevant member of the Korea Media Rating Board shall be excluded from the deliberation or resolution thereof: <Amended on Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Where the member or his or her spouse or ex-spouse has made an application under this Act to the Korea Media Rating Board, such as an application for film rating under Article 29(1) or an application for the rating of video products Article 50(1) or 50-2(3) (hereafter in this Article referred to as the "application");
  2. Where a person who has any right or liability held jointly with the member or his or her spouse or ex-spouse has made the application;
  3. Where a person who is or was in a family relationship with the member has made the application.
  (2) Any person who has made the application may, where there exists a good reason why it appears to him or her to be difficult to expect a fair resolution from a member, file an application for the challenge of the member, with a written explanation of such fact.
  (3) If a member falls under any such cause as provided for in any subparagraph of paragraph (1) or paragraph (2), he or she may voluntarily refrain from the deliberation and resolution of the case concerned.
  (4) Necessary matters concerning the exclusion, challenge and refrainment of members referred to in paragraphs (1) through (3) shall be determined by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 75 (Chairperson) (1) The chairperson and the vice chairperson of the Korea Media Rating Board shall be elected by mutual vote among the members of the Korea Media Rating Board.
  (2) The chairperson of the Korea Media Rating Board shall represent the Korea Media Rating Board and have overall control over the affairs of the Korea Media Rating Board.
  (3) In case the chairperson of the Korea Media Rating Board is unable to perform his or her duties for an inevitable reason, the vice chairperson shall act for him or her and, in case both the chairperson and the vice chairperson are unable to perform their duties, members of the Korea Media Rating Board in precedence of age shall act for them.
  (4) The members of the Korea Media Rating Board except the chairperson shall be non-standing.

Article 76 (Terms of office of members) (1) The terms of office of the members of the Korea Media Rating Board including its chairperson and vice chairperson shall be three years, respectively.
  (2) Where the office of any member of the Korea Media Rating Board is vacant, the supplementary member shall be commissioned pursuant to the procedures referred to in Article 73, and the term of office of the supplementary member shall be the remaining period of the term of office of his or her predecessor. <Amended on Dec. 3, 2019; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (3) The members of the Korea Media Rating Board including its chairperson and vice chairperson, whose terms of office have expired under paragraph (1) but successors are not yet appointed, shall continue to perform their duties until their successors are appointed. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 77 (Quorum) Decisions of the Korea Media Rating Board shall require the attendance of a majority of the total members and the concurrent vote of a majority of the members present; provided, any decision on the matter as prescribed in subparagraph 3 of Article 72 shall require the concurrent vote of a majority of the total members.

Article 78 (Opening of sessions to public) (1) Sessions of the Korea Media Rating Board shall be open to the public under the conditions as prescribed by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board; provided, sessions may be closed to the public by the decision of the Korea Media Rating Board if there exists any special reason, such as protection of trade secrets. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) The Korea Media Rating Board shall record the proceedings of sessions under the conditions as prescribed by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 79 (Sectional committees) (1) The Korea Media Rating Board may form sectional committees to carry out the matters which the Korea Media Rating Board has delegated to them in connection with the performance of its functions as prescribed in subparagraph 1 of Article 72. <Amended on Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) The Korea Media Rating Board shall form a post-control commission to perform the duties as prescribed in subparagraphs 2 and 2-2 of Article 72. <Amended on Sep. 27, 2022>
  (3) Such matters as may be necessary for the formation and operation of the sectional committees and the post-control commission shall be determined by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board.

Article 80 (Treatment of members and prohibition of concurrent office) (1) Standing members of the Korea Media Rating Board shall be paid remuneration within the limit of its budget under the conditions as prescribed by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board, while non-standing members of the Korea Media Rating Board shall be honorary but they may, nevertheless, be paid expenses necessary for performing their duties and other actual expenses under the conditions as prescribed by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (2) Except as otherwise provided in the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board, standing members of the Korea Media Rating Board may not concurrently hold another office for profit. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 81 (Grounds for disqualification of members) No person who falls under subparagraphs 1 through 3 of Article 12 may become members of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 82 (Independent exercise of duties and guarantee of status) (1) Members of the Korea Media Rating Board shall not be subject to any instructions or intervention in the exercise of their duties during their terms of office.
  (2) No member of the Korea Media Rating Board shall be removed from office or suffer any unfavorable treatment in his or her status against his or her will unless he or she falls under any one of the following subparagraphs:
  1. Where he or she falls under any disqualification as referred to in Article 81;
  2. Where he or she is unable to perform his or her duties for a long time due to any mental or physical impairment.
  (3) Where any member of the Korea Media Rating Board falls under paragraph (2)1, he or she shall be removed from office, and where he or she falls under subparagraph 2 of the said paragraph, the chairperson of the Korea Media Rating Board may recommend his or her removal from office to the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism, subject to a resolution by the Korea Media Rating Board. <Amended on May 8, 2009; Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 83 (Duties of Korea Media Rating Board involving rating) (1) The Korea Media Rating Board shall regularly survey public opinion on the rating of visual representations and reflect the results in the performance of the duties of rating, etc.
  (2) The Korea Media Rating Board may, if necessary to carry out its duties related to rating referred to in subparagraph 2 of Article 72, request motion picture enterprisers and video product business operators to submit related materials, and shall recommend the agency concerned to take necessary measures against an act of violation, if any. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  (3) Necessary matters concerning the submission of related materials under paragraph (2) shall be determined by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board. <Added on May 8, 2009>

Article 84 (Secretariat) (1) There shall be established a secretariat of the Korea Media Rating Board in order to assist the Korea Media Rating Board in doing clerical work.
  (2) A secretary general shall be assigned to the secretariat, and the chairperson of the Korea Media Rating Board shall appoint him or her with the approval of the Korea Media Rating Board.
  (3) Necessary matters for the formation and operation of the secretariat shall be determined by the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board.

Article 85 (Establishment, amendment and repeal of rules of Korea Media Rating Board) (1) In case the rules of the Korea Media Rating Board are to be established, amended or repealed, the Korea Media Rating Board shall give an advance notice of the bill of the rules or the bill of their amendment or repeal through the Official Gazette, etc. for a fixed period of not less than 20 days and shall, if the bill has been passed, announce it officially by publishing it through the Official Gazette, etc.
  (2) In case standards for rating are to be established or amended pursuant to Article 50(6), the Korea Media Rating Board shall hear opinions voiced by youth organizations, nonprofit private organizations, and academic or industrial circles. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 86 (Financial assistance from National Treasury) (1) The National Treasury may assist the Korea Media Rating Board with such expenses as may be required for its operation.
  (2) With respect to such project, etc. as may incur financial obligation on the National Treasury, the Korea Media Rating Board shall in advance consult with the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>

CHAPTER V SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 87 (Establishment of video product organization) (1) Video product business operators may establish a video product organization in order to promote a healthy and sound development of business.
  (2) The video product organization shall be a juristic person.
  (3) A person who desires to establish the video product organization shall obtain permission therefor from the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008>
  (4) The video product organization shall endeavor to ensure that a healthy and sound operational order may be maintained in doing business pertaining to the manufacture and distribution of video products.
  (5) The provisions on an incorporated association as referred to in the Civil Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the matters that this Act does not prescribe with respect to the video product organization.

Article 88 (Education for establishing order of video products distribution) The head of Si/Gun/Gu may, if deemed necessary to establish a healthy and sound order of distribution of video products, have the video service providing business operators undergo education within the limit of three hours per year under the conditions as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 89 (Assistance to exemplary video product business operators) (1) The head of Si/Gun/Gu may, if deemed necessary to establish a healthy and sound order of distribution of video products, designate exemplary video service providing business operators and thereby provide necessary assistance to them.
  (2) Necessary matters pertaining to the standards, procedures, etc. for designation as prescribed in paragraph (1) shall be determined by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 90 (Fees) (1) Deleted. <May 18, 2015>
  (2) Any of the following persons shall pay the Korea Media Rating Board the fees determined by the Korea Media Rating Board upon approval of the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism; provided, a person falling under subparagraph 1 or 4 who does not have a profit-making purpose shall be exempted from such fees: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Feb. 17, 2012; Oct. 16, 2018; Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. A person who applies for film rating pursuant to Article 29(1);
  2. A person who raises an objection pursuant to Article 31;
  3. A person who applies for a verification as to whether advertising or publicity materials of motion picture are harmful to youth pursuant to Article 32;
  4. A person who applies for the rating of video products pursuant to Article 50(1);
  4-2. A person who requests rating under Article 50-2(3);
  5. A person who applies for the confirmation of reproduced video products, etc. or the re-issuance of the certificate of confirmation pursuant to Article 51;
  6. A person who applies for the reclassification pursuant to Article 54(1);
  7. A person who applies for a verification as to whether advertising or publicity materials related to video products are harmful to youth pursuant to Article 66(1).
  (3) Any of the following persons shall pay fees, as prescribed by municipal ordinance of each Si/Gun/Gu (referring to an autonomous Gu): <Amended on May 8, 2009; Dec. 24, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. A person who applies for the registration or alteration of registered matters pursuant to Article 36(1);
  1-2. A person who files a report on a motion picture enterpriser or files a report on the change thereof pursuant to Article 26(1);
  1-3. A person who applies for the re-issuance of a certificate of report pursuant to Article 26(3);
  2. A person who files a report on a video product manufacturing business or a video product distributing business pursuant to Article 57(1);
  3. A person who applies for the registration of a video service providing business pursuant to Article 58(1);
  4. A person who files a report on alterations or files for registration of alterations pursuant to Article 61(1).
  (4) Where the Korea Media Rating Board intends to determine the fees referred to in paragraph (2), it shall consider opinions of relevant enterprisers. <Added on May 18, 2015>

Article 91 (Legal fiction as public officials for purposes of applying penalty provisions) The following persons shall be deemed public officials for purposes of applying penalty provisions pursuant to Articles 129 through 132 of the Criminal Act: <Amended on Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. Members of the Korean Film Council and the staff members of its secretariat;
  2. Members of the Korea Media Rating Board and the staff members of its secretariat;
  3. Members of the sectional committees of the Korea Media Rating Board and the members of its post-control commission;
  4. Persons engaged in the duties entrusted under Article 92(2) or (3).

Article 92 (Delegation and entrustment of authority) (1) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may delegate part of his or her authority under this Act to a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on May 8, 2009>
  (2) The Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism may entrust the Korean Film Council, the Korean Film Archive, the Korea Media Rating Board, or a specialized institution or organization related to the film and video culture and the video industry with some of its duties, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Added on Sep. 27, 2022>
  (3) The Korean Film Council may entrust part of its duties to the institutions or organizations established for the purpose of promoting the film and video culture and the film and video industry, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023>

CHAPTER VI PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 93 (Penalty provisions) Any person who has run business in violation of a measure taken pursuant to each subparagraph of Article 70(1) shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than five years, or by a fine not exceeding 50 million won. <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>

Article 94 (Penalty provisions) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than three years, or by a fine not exceeding 30 million won: <Amended on May 8, 2009; Feb. 17, 2012; May 18, 2015; Oct. 22, 2024>
  1. A person who screens a motion picture which has not been rated, in violation of Article 29(3);
  2. A person who admits any youth who is prohibited from viewing a restricted motion picture to his or her movie theater to view such motion picture, in violation of Article 29(5);
  3. A person who screens any restricted motion picture at a place or establishment that is not a restricted-screening theater, in violation of Article 43(1);
  4. A person who has made any restricted motion picture into other visual representations or screened, sold, transmitted, rented or offered such produced visual representations for viewing, in violation of Article 43(2);
  5. A person who offers a video product with a restricted rating for viewing at a place or facilities, other than a video-viewing mini-theater for video products with a restricted rating, in violation of Article 53-2(1);
  6. A person who distributes a video product with a restricted rating, in violation of Article 53-2(2);
  7. A person who permits a youth to be admitted into a video-viewing establishment, a video-viewing mini-theater for video products with a restricted rating or into a business establishment for providing multiple types of visual materials, in violation of Article 62(1)4.

Article 95 (Penalty provisions) Any of the following persons shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than 2 years, or by a fine not exceeding 20 million won: <Amended on May 8, 2009; Oct. 16, 2018; Aug. 8, 2023; Oct. 22, 2024; Mar. 25, 2025>
  1. A person who has screened a motion picture, in violation of Article 29(6);
  2. A person who has posted the advertising or promotion materials related to any restricted motion picture or has them available outside a restricted-screening theater, in violation of Article 33;
  3. A person who has screened any such motion picture referred to in Article 29(2)1 through 4 in a restricted-screening theater, in violation of Article 43(3);
  4. A person who has failed to comply with an order given to suspend business under Article 45;
  5. A person who has obtained the rating of video products under Article 50(1) or has received the confirmation of reproduction, distribution, etc. under Article 51(1) without any legitimate right to such video products in any false or other unjust manner;
  6. A person who has manufactured or distributed illegal video products, offered them for viewing, or to this end, displayed or kept them, in violation of Article 53(1);
  7. A person who has sold, purchased or donated the rating certificate and the certificate of confirmation, in violation of Article 53(4);
  8. A person who offers a video product under Article 50(3)1 through 4 to the general public for their viewing at a video-viewing mini-theater for video products with a restricted rating, in violation of Article 53-2(3);
  9. A person who has engaged in video service providing business without registering the business, in violation of Article 58(1);
  10. A person who has failed to abide by the matters to be observed, in violation of Article 62(1)3;
  11. A person who posts the advertising or promotion materials of video products with a restricted rating at places, other than the inside of a video-viewing mini-theater for video products with a restricted rating, or makes them visible outside a video- viewing mini-theater for video products with a restricted rating, in violation of Article 66(5);
  12. A person who has continued business, in violation of an order to suspend such business under Article 67(2).

Article 95-2 (Penalty provisions) A person who allows a youth to enter a video-viewing mini-theater establishment in violation of Article 62(1)2 shall be punished by imprisonment with labor for not more than 1 year or by a fine not exceeding 10 million won.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 25, 2025]

Article 96 (Penalty provisions) A person who falls under any one of the following subparagraphs shall be punished by a fine not exceeding 10 million won: <Amended on Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. A person who has run business without reporting, in violation of Article 57;
  2. A person who has continued business, in violation of an order to suspend such business under Article 67(1);
  3. A person who has refused, obstructed, or evaded any action taken by the relevant public official pursuant to Article 70(1) or (3).

Article 96-2 (Penalty provisions) A person who enters into a labor contract in violation of Article 3-4 shall be punished by a fine not exceeding five million won.
  [This Article Added on May 18, 2015]

Article 97 (Joint penalty provisions) Where a representative of a corporation, or an agent, employee or other servant of a corporation or individual commits an offense under any one of Articles 93 through 95, 95-2, 96, and 96-2 in connection with the business of the corporation or individual, not only shall such offender be punished accordingly, but the corporation or the individual shall also be punished by the fine prescribed in the relevant Article; provided, the foregoing sentence shall not apply to where such corporation or individual has not been negligent in giving due attention and supervision concerning the relevant duties to prevent such offense. <Amended on May 18, 2015; Mar. 25, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 8, 2009]

Article 98 (Administrative fines) (1) Any of the following persons shall be subject to imposition of an administrative fines not exceeding 50 million won: <Amended on May 18, 2015; Sep. 27, 2022; Aug. 8, 2023>
  1. A person who admits any person prohibited from viewing a motion picture with a film rating provided for in Article 29(2)2 through 4 to his or her movie theater to view such motion picture, in violation of Article 29(4) or (5);
  2. A person who distributes or posts advertising or publicity materials without obtaining verification as to whether they are harmful to youth or offers such materials for public viewing through the information and communications networks, in violation of Article 32(1);
  3. A person who distributes or posts advertising or publicity materials verified as harmful to youth, in violation of Article 32(2);
  3-2. A person who fails to comply with an order to suspend business under Article 50-6(1);
  4. A person who offers video products for viewing, in violation of Article 53(2);
  5. A person who fails to obtain verification as to whether advertising or publicity materials are harmful to youth, in violation of Article 66(1);
  6. A person who distributes or posts advertising or publicity materials which are verified as harmful to youth, in violation of Article 66(2).
  (2) Any of the following persons shall be subject to imposition of an administrative fine not exceeding 10 million won: <Amended on Jan. 26, 2007; May 8, 2009; May 18, 2015; Dec. 20, 2016; Dec. 24, 2018>
  1. Deleted; <May 18, 2015>
  1-2. A person who operates business manufacturing, importing, distributing or screening motion pictures without reporting thereon, in violation of Article 26(1);
  2. A person who fails to submit a motion picture film, etc. in violation of Article 35(1);
  3. A person who establishes and operates a movie theater without having such movie theater registered, in violation of Article 36(1);
  4. A person who fails to formulate, report on or supplement a safety-precaution plan, in violation of the former part of Article 37(1) or paragraph (2) of the same Article;
  4-2. A person who fails to take disaster prevention measures in violation of Article 37(4);
  5. A person who transmits the data related to the matters prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism, such as the number of viewers entering the relevant movie theater and the amount of sales of admission tickets through the integrated computer network for processing movie theater admission tickets, with intentional omission or manipulation, in violation of Article 39(3);
  6. A person who fails to report or makes a false report, in violation of Article 41;
  7. A person who allows a person without the national technical qualifications for motion picture projection to screen any motion picture, in violation of Article 44;
  8. A person who fails to report or obtain registration of any alteration, in violation of Article 61(1);
  9. A person who fails to indicate a rating or content information of a video product or misstates such rating or content information, in violation of Article 65(1) or (2).
  (3) Any of the following persons shall be subject the imposition of an administrative fine not exceeding three million won: <Amended on May 18, 2015; Dec. 24, 2018>
  1. A person who fails to report on any alteration, in violation of the latter part of Article 26(1);
  2. A person who has fails to register alteration of any registered matter involving a movie theater, in violation of the latter part of Article 36(1);
  3. A person who fails to report, in violation of Article 46(1) or (2) or 63(1) or (2).

Article 99 (Imposition of administrative fines) Administrative fines under Article 98 shall be imposed and collected by the Minister of Culture, Sports and Tourism or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on May 8, 2009]

